window.FORGE_SOURCE = {"files":[{"path":"src/main.c","title":"The read-parse-execute loop. Every command you type passes through here.","group":"Shell core","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"common.h\"","#include \"parser.h\"","#include \"executor.h\"","#include \"jobs.h\"","#include <ctype.h>","","static void print_prompt(void) {","    if (g_shell.is_interactive) {","        if (isatty(STDOUT_FILENO)) {","            printf(\"%sforge>%s \", COLOR_BOLD COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_RESET);","        } else {","            printf(\"forge> \");","        }","        fflush(stdout);","    }","}","","static bool is_all_whitespace(const char *str) {","    while (*str) {","        if (!isspace((unsigned char)*str)) {","            return false;","        }","        str++;","    }","    return true;","}","","int main(int argc, char **argv) {","    (void)argc;","    (void)argv;","","    shell_init();","","    char *line = NULL;","    size_t len = 0;","","    while (1) {","        check_and_notify_jobs();","        print_prompt();","","        ssize_t nread = getline(&line, &len, stdin);","        if (nread == -1) {","            /* EOF reached (e.g. Ctrl+D or end of pipe) */","            if (g_shell.is_interactive) {","                printf(\"\\n\");","            }","            break;","        }","","        if (is_all_whitespace(line)) {","            continue;","        }","","        JobPipeline *pipeline = parse_pipeline(line);","        if (pipeline) {","            execute_pipeline(pipeline);","            free_pipeline(pipeline);","        }","    }","","    free(line);","    return g_shell.last_exit_status;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p>The entry point includes the headers for the three parts it drives: the parser (text to structures), the executor (structures to processes) and the job table (for background-job notices). <code>common.h</code> brings the shared basics, and <code>ctype.h</code> is for <code>isspace()</code>.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":17,"html":"<p>Prints <code>forge&gt; </code> only when the shell is interactive, meaning stdin is a terminal. When the tests pipe commands into ForgeShell there's no human to read a prompt, so none is printed. The prompt is only colored when stdout is also a terminal, so escape codes never end up in a file or pipe.</p><p><code>fflush(stdout)</code> matters: stdout is line-buffered on a terminal and the prompt has no newline, so without the flush it would sit in the buffer and never appear.</p>"},{"start":18,"end":27,"html":"<p>A tiny helper so a blank line (or just spaces and a newline) is skipped before it reaches the parser. The <code>(unsigned char)</code> cast is the correct way to call <code>isspace()</code>: passing a negative <code>char</code> (bytes above 127 on most platforms) is undefined behaviour.</p>"},{"start":28,"end":33,"html":"<p><code>argc</code> and <code>argv</code> are unused: ForgeShell has no command-line flags and always reads commands from stdin. The <code>(void)</code> casts silence the unused-parameter warnings that <code>-Wextra</code> would otherwise print.</p><p><code>shell_init()</code> runs once. It works out whether we're interactive, takes control of the terminal, sets up signal handling and empties the job table. See <code>src/executor.c</code>.</p>"},{"start":34,"end":36,"html":"<p><code>getline()</code> manages the buffer for us. Starting with <code>NULL</code> and <code>0</code> tells it to <code>malloc</code> a buffer, and it will <code>realloc</code> as needed for longer lines. The same buffer is reused on every pass and freed once at the end.</p>"},{"start":37,"end":41,"html":"<p>The heart of the shell. Each pass:</p><p>1. <code>check_and_notify_jobs()</code> reaps any finished or stopped children without blocking and prints <code>[1]+ Done ...</code> lines. It runs <em>before</em> the prompt, which is why Bash-style shells report finished background jobs just before the next prompt rather than mid-typing.</p><p>2. Print the prompt and block in <code>getline()</code> until a full line arrives.</p>"},{"start":42,"end":49,"html":"<p><code>getline()</code> returns -1 at end of input: Ctrl+D on an empty line at a terminal, or the end of a piped script. Printing a newline keeps the user's real shell prompt from landing on the same line as <code>forge&gt;</code>.</p>"},{"start":50,"end":53,"html":"<p>Empty lines are skipped without touching the parser or the job table.</p>"},{"start":54,"end":60,"html":"<p>Parse, run, free. <code>parse_pipeline()</code> returns <code>NULL</code> both for syntax errors (after printing a message) and for input that has no command, so in both cases we simply loop. Freeing the pipeline right after execution means the shell's own memory stays flat no matter how many commands you run.</p>"},{"start":61,"end":63,"html":"<p>On EOF the loop breaks, the line buffer is released, and the shell exits with the status of the last command. That's how <code>exit</code> codes propagate when ForgeShell runs a script from a pipe.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/parser.c","title":"Turns a raw line of text into a pipeline of commands, in two passes: tokenize, then parse.","group":"Shell core","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"parser.h\"","#include <ctype.h>","","typedef enum {","    TOKEN_WORD,","    TOKEN_PIPE,           /* | */","    TOKEN_IN_REDIR,       /* < */","    TOKEN_OUT_REDIR,      /* > */","    TOKEN_APPEND_REDIR,   /* >> */","    TOKEN_ERR_REDIR,      /* 2> */","    TOKEN_BACKGROUND,     /* & */","    TOKEN_EOF","} TokenType;","","typedef struct {","    TokenType type;","    char *text;","} Token;","","typedef struct {","    Token *tokens;","    size_t count;","    size_t capacity;","} TokenList;","","static void token_list_init(TokenList *list) {","    list->capacity = 16;","    list->count = 0;","    list->tokens = xmalloc(list->capacity * sizeof(Token));","}","","static void token_list_add(TokenList *list, TokenType type, const char *text) {","    if (list->count >= list->capacity) {","        list->capacity *= 2;","        list->tokens = xrealloc(list->tokens, list->capacity * sizeof(Token));","    }","    list->tokens[list->count].type = type;","    list->tokens[list->count].text = text ? xstrdup(text) : NULL;","    list->count++;","}","","static void token_list_free(TokenList *list) {","    if (!list || !list->tokens) return;","    for (size_t i = 0; i < list->count; i++) {","        if (list->tokens[i].text) {","            free(list->tokens[i].text);","        }","    }","    free(list->tokens);","    list->tokens = NULL;","    list->count = 0;","    list->capacity = 0;","}","","/* Lexical scanner: convert raw line into token list */","static bool tokenize(const char *input, TokenList *list) {","    token_list_init(list);","    const char *p = input;","","    while (*p) {","        /* Skip whitespace */","        if (isspace((unsigned char)*p)) {","            p++;","            continue;","        }","","        /* Check for operators */","        if (*p == '|') {","            token_list_add(list, TOKEN_PIPE, \"|\");","            p++;","            continue;","        }","","        if (*p == '&') {","            token_list_add(list, TOKEN_BACKGROUND, \"&\");","            p++;","            continue;","        }","","        if (*p == '<') {","            token_list_add(list, TOKEN_IN_REDIR, \"<\");","            p++;","            continue;","        }","","        if (*p == '>') {","            if (*(p + 1) == '>') {","                token_list_add(list, TOKEN_APPEND_REDIR, \">>\");","                p += 2;","            } else {","                token_list_add(list, TOKEN_OUT_REDIR, \">\");","                p++;","            }","            continue;","        }","","        if (*p == '2' && *(p + 1) == '>') {","            token_list_add(list, TOKEN_ERR_REDIR, \"2>\");","            p += 2;","            continue;","        }","","        /* Parse word (including quotes and escapes) */","        size_t buf_cap = 64;","        size_t buf_len = 0;","        char *buf = xmalloc(buf_cap);","","        bool in_word = true;","        while (in_word && *p) {","            if (*p == '\\'') {","                /* Single quotes: preserve everything literally until closing quote */","                p++;","                while (*p && *p != '\\'') {","                    if (buf_len + 1 >= buf_cap) {","                        buf_cap *= 2;","                        buf = xrealloc(buf, buf_cap);","                    }","                    buf[buf_len++] = *p++;","                }","                if (*p != '\\'') {","                    forge_error(\"syntax error: unclosed single quote\");","                    free(buf);","                    token_list_free(list);","                    return false;","                }","                p++; /* consume closing single quote */","            } else if (*p == '\"') {","                /* Double quotes: preserve spaces, handle basic escapes */","                p++;","                while (*p && *p != '\"') {","                    if (*p == '\\\\' && (*(p + 1) == '\"' || *(p + 1) == '\\\\')) {","                        p++;","                    }","                    if (buf_len + 1 >= buf_cap) {","                        buf_cap *= 2;","                        buf = xrealloc(buf, buf_cap);","                    }","                    buf[buf_len++] = *p++;","                }","                if (*p != '\"') {","                    forge_error(\"syntax error: unclosed double quote\");","                    free(buf);","                    token_list_free(list);","                    return false;","                }","                p++; /* consume closing double quote */","            } else if (*p == '\\\\' && *(p + 1) != '\\0') {","                /* Escape next character */","                p++;","                if (buf_len + 1 >= buf_cap) {","                    buf_cap *= 2;","                    buf = xrealloc(buf, buf_cap);","                }","                buf[buf_len++] = *p++;","            } else if (isspace((unsigned char)*p) || *p == '|' || *p == '&' || *p == '<' || *p == '>' ||","                       (*p == '2' && *(p + 1) == '>')) {","                /* End of unquoted word */","                in_word = false;","            } else {","                if (buf_len + 1 >= buf_cap) {","                    buf_cap *= 2;","                    buf = xrealloc(buf, buf_cap);","                }","                buf[buf_len++] = *p++;","            }","        }","","        buf[buf_len] = '\\0';","        token_list_add(list, TOKEN_WORD, buf);","        free(buf);","    }","","    token_list_add(list, TOKEN_EOF, NULL);","    return true;","}","","static Command *create_command(void) {","    Command *cmd = xmalloc(sizeof(Command));","    cmd->argc = 0;","    cmd->argv = xmalloc(8 * sizeof(char *));","    cmd->argv[0] = NULL;","    cmd->input_file = NULL;","    cmd->output_file = NULL;","    cmd->append_output = false;","    cmd->error_file = NULL;","    cmd->next = NULL;","    return cmd;","}","","static void command_add_arg(Command *cmd, const char *arg) {","    cmd->argv = xrealloc(cmd->argv, (cmd->argc + 2) * sizeof(char *));","    cmd->argv[cmd->argc] = xstrdup(arg);","    cmd->argc++;","    cmd->argv[cmd->argc] = NULL;","}","","static void free_command(Command *cmd) {","    if (!cmd) return;","    if (cmd->argv) {","        for (int i = 0; i < cmd->argc; i++) {","            if (cmd->argv[i]) free(cmd->argv[i]);","        }","        free(cmd->argv);","    }","    if (cmd->input_file) free(cmd->input_file);","    if (cmd->output_file) free(cmd->output_file);","    if (cmd->error_file) free(cmd->error_file);","    free(cmd);","}","","void free_pipeline(JobPipeline *pipeline) {","    if (!pipeline) return;","    Command *curr = pipeline->first_cmd;","    while (curr) {","        Command *next = curr->next;","        free_command(curr);","        curr = next;","    }","    if (pipeline->raw_line) {","        free(pipeline->raw_line);","    }","    free(pipeline);","}","","JobPipeline *parse_pipeline(const char *input_line) {","    if (!input_line) return NULL;","","    TokenList list;","    if (!tokenize(input_line, &list)) {","        return NULL;","    }","","    if (list.count <= 1 || list.tokens[0].type == TOKEN_EOF) {","        token_list_free(&list);","        return NULL;","    }","","    JobPipeline *pipeline = xmalloc(sizeof(JobPipeline));","    pipeline->first_cmd = NULL;","    pipeline->is_background = false;","    pipeline->raw_line = xstrdup(input_line);","","    /* Strip trailing newline from raw_line for clean jobs output */","    size_t raw_len = strlen(pipeline->raw_line);","    if (raw_len > 0 && pipeline->raw_line[raw_len - 1] == '\\n') {","        pipeline->raw_line[raw_len - 1] = '\\0';","    }","","    Command *current_cmd = create_command();","    pipeline->first_cmd = current_cmd;","","    size_t i = 0;","    while (i < list.count && list.tokens[i].type != TOKEN_EOF) {","        TokenType type = list.tokens[i].type;","","        if (type == TOKEN_WORD) {","            command_add_arg(current_cmd, list.tokens[i].text);","            i++;","        } else if (type == TOKEN_IN_REDIR) {","            i++;","            if (i >= list.count || list.tokens[i].type != TOKEN_WORD) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error near unexpected token '<'\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            if (current_cmd->input_file) free(current_cmd->input_file);","            current_cmd->input_file = xstrdup(list.tokens[i].text);","            i++;","        } else if (type == TOKEN_OUT_REDIR || type == TOKEN_APPEND_REDIR) {","            bool append = (type == TOKEN_APPEND_REDIR);","            i++;","            if (i >= list.count || list.tokens[i].type != TOKEN_WORD) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error near unexpected token '>'\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            if (current_cmd->output_file) free(current_cmd->output_file);","            current_cmd->output_file = xstrdup(list.tokens[i].text);","            current_cmd->append_output = append;","            i++;","        } else if (type == TOKEN_ERR_REDIR) {","            i++;","            if (i >= list.count || list.tokens[i].type != TOKEN_WORD) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error near unexpected token '2>'\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            if (current_cmd->error_file) free(current_cmd->error_file);","            current_cmd->error_file = xstrdup(list.tokens[i].text);","            i++;","        } else if (type == TOKEN_PIPE) {","            if (current_cmd->argc == 0) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error near unexpected token '|'\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            i++;","            if (i >= list.count || list.tokens[i].type == TOKEN_EOF || list.tokens[i].type == TOKEN_PIPE) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error near unexpected token '|'\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            Command *next_cmd = create_command();","            current_cmd->next = next_cmd;","            current_cmd = next_cmd;","        } else if (type == TOKEN_BACKGROUND) {","            i++;","            /* & must be the last meaningful token */","            if (i < list.count && list.tokens[i].type != TOKEN_EOF) {","                forge_error(\"syntax error: '&' must be at end of command\");","                free_pipeline(pipeline);","                token_list_free(&list);","                return NULL;","            }","            pipeline->is_background = true;","            break;","        }","    }","","    token_list_free(&list);","","    if (!pipeline->first_cmd || pipeline->first_cmd->argc == 0) {","        free_pipeline(pipeline);","        return NULL;","    }","","    /* Every later stage needs a command too: \"ls | > out.txt\" has none after","     * the pipe, and running it would mean execvp(NULL). */","    for (Command *c = pipeline->first_cmd->next; c; c = c->next) {","        if (c->argc == 0) {","            forge_error(\"syntax error: missing command after '|'\");","            free_pipeline(pipeline);","            return NULL;","        }","    }","","    return pipeline;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":3,"html":"<p>The parser is split into two stages, like a tiny compiler. The <strong>lexer</strong> (<code>tokenize</code>) turns characters into tokens: words and operators. The <strong>parser</strong> (<code>parse_pipeline</code>) turns tokens into structures the executor can run. Keeping them separate means quote handling lives in exactly one place, and the parser never has to think about characters.</p>"},{"start":4,"end":14,"html":"<p>The seven token kinds ForgeShell understands, plus <code>TOKEN_EOF</code> as an end marker so the parser never has to check array bounds and token type separately. Note there's no token for <code>;</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, <code>$</code> or <code>*</code>: those features don't exist in this shell, and the lexer treats those characters as ordinary word characters.</p>"},{"start":15,"end":19,"html":"<p>A token is a type plus its text. Operators carry their own text (<code>\"|\"</code>, <code>\"&gt;&gt;\"</code>) only for error messages. Words carry the final, unquoted text: by the time a word becomes a token, its quotes have already been removed.</p>"},{"start":20,"end":25,"html":"<p>A growable array (<code>count</code> used, <code>capacity</code> allocated). Simpler and faster than a linked list for something that's built once and read front to back.</p>"},{"start":26,"end":31,"html":"<p>Starts with room for 16 tokens. Most command lines fit without any reallocation.</p>"},{"start":32,"end":41,"html":"<p>The classic doubling strategy: when the array is full, double its capacity. Doubling makes appends <em>amortised O(1)</em>: over n appends the total copying work is under 2n. Each token's text is copied with <code>xstrdup</code>, so the list owns its strings and the caller can free its buffer straight away.</p>"},{"start":42,"end":54,"html":"<p>Frees every token's text, then the array, and resets the fields so a double free can't happen. It's called on every exit path, including syntax errors halfway through.</p>"},{"start":55,"end":59,"html":"<p><code>tokenize</code> walks the input with a single pointer <code>p</code>. There's no <code>strtok()</code>: <code>strtok</code> modifies the input, keeps hidden global state and can't understand quotes, so <code>\"a | b\"</code> would be split at the pipe.</p>"},{"start":60,"end":67,"html":"<p>Whitespace between tokens is skipped. Whitespace <em>inside</em> quotes is handled further down and never reaches this check.</p>"},{"start":68,"end":85,"html":"<p>Single-character operators. Each check emits a token, advances one character and restarts the loop. Because these checks come before word scanning, <code>ls|wc</code> with no spaces still splits into three tokens.</p>"},{"start":86,"end":96,"html":"<p>One character of lookahead (<code>*(p + 1)</code>) tells <code>&gt;</code> apart from <code>&gt;&gt;</code>. Checking <code>&gt;&gt;</code> first is the standard \"longest match\" rule lexers use.</p>"},{"start":97,"end":102,"html":"<p><code>2&gt;</code> only counts as an operator when it starts a token. There's a subtle quirk, though: the word scanner below also stops at <code>2&gt;</code>, so <code>echo a2&gt;f</code> lexes as <code>echo</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>2&gt;</code>, <code>f</code>. Bash would read <code>a2</code> as the word and <code>&gt;</code> as the redirect. A fix would be to only treat <code>2&gt;</code> as an operator when it's at the start of a word.</p>"},{"start":103,"end":109,"html":"<p>Anything else starts a word. The word is collected into a growable buffer (<code>buf</code>, starting at 64 bytes and doubling), because quotes mean the token text isn't simply a slice of the input.</p><p>A single word can mix styles: <code>he'll'\"o\"</code> becomes <code>hello</code>. That's why the inner loop keeps going until it hits an unquoted space or operator.</p>"},{"start":110,"end":126,"html":"<p><strong>Single quotes</strong>: everything up to the next <code>'</code> is copied byte for byte, with no escapes at all. <code>'$HOME | x'</code> stays exactly that. If the input ends first, it's a syntax error, and both the half-built buffer and the token list are freed before returning <code>false</code>.</p>"},{"start":127,"end":146,"html":"<p><strong>Double quotes</strong>: spaces and operators are kept, but a backslash can escape <code>\"</code> or <code>\\</code>. Any other backslash is kept as is, so <code>\"a\\nb\"</code> reaches <code>printf</code> as the four characters <code>a\\nb</code>, and that's why <code>printf \"a\\nb\"</code> works in the demo. There's no <code>$VAR</code> expansion, since the shell doesn't have variables.</p>"},{"start":147,"end":154,"html":"<p><strong>Unquoted backslash</strong>: the next character is taken literally, so <code>a\\ b</code> is one word containing a space and <code>\\|</code> is a literal pipe character. One edge case: the line from <code>getline()</code> still ends in <code>\\n</code>, so a backslash typed at the end of a line escapes that newline and puts it inside the word. Bash would treat it as a line continuation instead.</p>"},{"start":155,"end":158,"html":"<p>An unquoted space or operator ends the word. The operator is <em>not</em> consumed here: the outer loop sees it next and turns it into its own token.</p>"},{"start":159,"end":167,"html":"<p>Ordinary characters are appended. Every append path checks <code>buf_len + 1 &gt;= buf_cap</code> first, so there's always room for the final <code>'\\0'</code>.</p>"},{"start":168,"end":172,"html":"<p>The finished word becomes a <code>TOKEN_WORD</code>. <code>token_list_add</code> copies it, so the scratch buffer is freed right away.</p><p>Note that an empty quoted string <code>\"\"</code> still produces a word token with empty text, so <code>echo \"\"</code> passes an empty argument just like Bash.</p>"},{"start":173,"end":176,"html":"<p>The sentinel token. Every parser loop stops at <code>TOKEN_EOF</code>, which removes a whole class of off-by-one bugs.</p>"},{"start":177,"end":189,"html":"<p>A fresh command with an empty, <code>NULL</code>-terminated <code>argv</code>. <code>execvp()</code> requires <code>argv</code> to end with a <code>NULL</code> pointer, and keeping that true at all times means the command is always ready to run.</p>"},{"start":190,"end":196,"html":"<p>Grows <code>argv</code> by exactly one slot each time (<code>argc + 2</code>: one for the new argument, one for the <code>NULL</code> terminator). Unlike the token list it doesn't double, which is fine because commands have few arguments. <code>realloc</code> may move the array, which is why the result is assigned back to <code>cmd-&gt;argv</code>.</p>"},{"start":197,"end":210,"html":"<p>Frees everything a command owns: each argument, the array, and the three redirect file names. <code>free(NULL)</code> is legal, but the explicit checks make the ownership easy to read.</p>"},{"start":211,"end":224,"html":"<p>Walks the linked list of commands, saving <code>next</code> <em>before</em> freeing the current node (reading <code>curr-&gt;next</code> after <code>free(curr)</code> would be a use-after-free), then frees the saved raw line and the pipeline itself.</p>"},{"start":225,"end":232,"html":"<p>The parser. It returns <code>NULL</code> for \"nothing to run\", whether that's a syntax error (a message is printed) or input that holds only whitespace.</p>"},{"start":233,"end":237,"html":"<p>A list with only the EOF sentinel means there was nothing on the line.</p>"},{"start":238,"end":248,"html":"<p>The pipeline keeps a copy of the original line (<code>raw_line</code>), minus its newline, purely for display: <code>jobs</code> prints it and <code>fg</code> echoes it.</p>"},{"start":249,"end":255,"html":"<p>The parser is a simple state machine: there's always a \"current command\" that words and redirects attach to, and a <code>|</code> starts a new one. This is one-token lookahead parsing. The grammar is simple enough that it needs no recursion and no tree.</p>"},{"start":256,"end":258,"html":"<p>Words become arguments of the current command, in order. Words can come after redirects too: <code>sort &gt; out.txt -r</code> is valid and gives <code>sort -r</code> with output to <code>out.txt</code>.</p>"},{"start":259,"end":269,"html":"<p>A redirect operator must be followed by a word, the file name. If it isn't (<code>cat &lt;</code> or <code>cat &lt; |</code>), that's a syntax error, and <em>everything</em> allocated so far is freed before returning. If a command has two <code>&lt;</code> redirects, the last one wins, and the earlier name is freed so it doesn't leak.</p>"},{"start":270,"end":282,"html":"<p><code>&gt;</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;</code> share one branch and differ only in the <code>append_output</code> flag. The executor turns that flag into <code>O_APPEND</code> or <code>O_TRUNC</code>.</p>"},{"start":283,"end":293,"html":"<p><code>2&gt;</code> sends standard error to a file. There's no <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> (merging stderr into stdout): the lexer has no token for <code>&amp;1</code>.</p>"},{"start":294,"end":310,"html":"<p>Two syntax checks for pipes: a pipe can't come first (<code>| wc</code>), and it can't be last or doubled (<code>ls |</code>, <code>ls | | wc</code>). Then a new command is created and linked onto the end of the list, and parsing continues into it.</p>"},{"start":311,"end":316,"html":"<p><code>&amp;</code> is only allowed at the end, so <code>a &amp; b</code> is an error here (in Bash it would run <code>a</code> in the background and then <code>b</code>). It sets one flag for the whole pipeline, and the executor uses it to decide whether to wait.</p>"},{"start":317,"end":343,"html":"<p>The tokens are no longer needed: every string the commands use was copied out of them.</p><p>Two final checks. A line with only redirects (<code>&gt; out.txt</code>) returns <code>NULL</code> quietly, so nothing runs. Bash would create an empty <code>out.txt</code>, but here nothing happens.</p><p>The second check was added today. A <em>later</em> stage of a pipeline with no command, as in <code>ls | &gt; out.txt</code>, used to slip through. The executor then called <code>execvp(NULL, ...)</code> and the child crashed with a segmentation fault. Now it's a syntax error, and <code>tests/test_parser.c</code> checks for it.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/executor.c","title":"Starts processes: shell setup, redirections, builtins, and the fork/pipe/exec loop for pipelines.","group":"Shell core","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"executor.h\"","","ShellState g_shell = {","    .last_exit_status = 0,","    .is_interactive = false,","    .shell_pgid = 0,","    .shell_terminal = STDIN_FILENO","};","","void shell_init(void) {","    g_shell.shell_terminal = STDIN_FILENO;","    g_shell.is_interactive = isatty(g_shell.shell_terminal);","    g_shell.last_exit_status = 0;","","    if (g_shell.is_interactive) {","        /* Loop until we are in the foreground process group */","        while (tcgetpgrp(g_shell.shell_terminal) != (g_shell.shell_pgid = getpgrp())) {","            kill(-g_shell.shell_pgid, SIGTTIN);","        }","","        /* Set up signal dispositions for interactive shell */","        signals_init_shell();","","        /* Place shell into its own process group. A session leader (e.g. a","         * shell started directly by a terminal emulator) already leads its","         * group, and setpgid() on a session leader fails with EPERM. */","        g_shell.shell_pgid = getpid();","        if (getpgrp() != g_shell.shell_pgid &&","            setpgid(g_shell.shell_pgid, g_shell.shell_pgid) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"setpgid\");","        }","","        /* Acquire terminal control */","        tcsetpgrp(g_shell.shell_terminal, g_shell.shell_pgid);","","        /* Save default terminal attributes */","        tcgetattr(g_shell.shell_terminal, &g_shell.shell_tmodes);","    }","","    jobs_init();","}","","int apply_redirections(const Command *cmd) {","    if (!cmd) return 0;","","    /* Stdin redirection: < input_file */","    if (cmd->input_file) {","        int fd = open(cmd->input_file, O_RDONLY);","        if (fd < 0) {","            forge_perror(cmd->input_file);","            return -1;","        }","        if (dup2(fd, STDIN_FILENO) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"dup2 stdin\");","            close(fd);","            return -1;","        }","        close(fd);","    }","","    /* Stdout redirection: > or >> output_file */","    if (cmd->output_file) {","        int flags = O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | (cmd->append_output ? O_APPEND : O_TRUNC);","        int fd = open(cmd->output_file, flags, 0644);","        if (fd < 0) {","            forge_perror(cmd->output_file);","            return -1;","        }","        if (dup2(fd, STDOUT_FILENO) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"dup2 stdout\");","            close(fd);","            return -1;","        }","        close(fd);","    }","","    /* Stderr redirection: 2> error_file */","    if (cmd->error_file) {","        int fd = open(cmd->error_file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);","        if (fd < 0) {","            forge_perror(cmd->error_file);","            return -1;","        }","        if (dup2(fd, STDERR_FILENO) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"dup2 stderr\");","            close(fd);","            return -1;","        }","        close(fd);","    }","","    return 0;","}","","static int execute_single_builtin(Command *cmd) {","    /* Save standard file descriptors */","    int saved_stdin = dup(STDIN_FILENO);","    int saved_stdout = dup(STDOUT_FILENO);","    int saved_stderr = dup(STDERR_FILENO);","","    int status = 0;","    if (apply_redirections(cmd) < 0) {","        status = 1;","    } else {","        status = execute_builtin(cmd->argc, cmd->argv);","    }","","    /* Restore file descriptors */","    dup2(saved_stdin, STDIN_FILENO);","    dup2(saved_stdout, STDOUT_FILENO);","    dup2(saved_stderr, STDERR_FILENO);","","    close(saved_stdin);","    close(saved_stdout);","    close(saved_stderr);","","    g_shell.last_exit_status = status;","    return status;","}","","int execute_pipeline(JobPipeline *pipeline) {","    if (!pipeline || !pipeline->first_cmd) {","        return 0;","    }","","    /* Count commands in the pipeline */","    int num_cmds = 0;","    for (Command *c = pipeline->first_cmd; c; c = c->next) {","        num_cmds++;","    }","","    /* If it's a single command and it's a builtin, run in-process (unless background) */","    if (num_cmds == 1 && !pipeline->is_background && is_builtin(pipeline->first_cmd->argv[0])) {","        return execute_single_builtin(pipeline->first_cmd);","    }","","    /* Allocate Job entry for tracking */","    Job *job = job_create(0, pipeline->raw_line);","","    pid_t pgid = 0;","    int prev_pipe_read = -1;","    Command *cmd = pipeline->first_cmd;","","    for (int i = 0; i < num_cmds; i++, cmd = cmd->next) {","        int pipefds[2] = {-1, -1};","        if (i < num_cmds - 1) {","            if (pipe(pipefds) < 0) {","                forge_perror(\"pipe\");","                g_shell.last_exit_status = 1;","                return 1;","            }","        }","","        pid_t pid = fork();","        if (pid < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"fork\");","            if (pipefds[0] != -1) {","                close(pipefds[0]);","                close(pipefds[1]);","            }","            if (prev_pipe_read != -1) {","                close(prev_pipe_read);","            }","            g_shell.last_exit_status = 1;","            return 1;","        }","","        if (pid == 0) {","            /* Child process */","            pid_t my_pid = getpid();","            if (pgid == 0) pgid = my_pid;","","            /* Put child in job's process group */","            setpgid(0, pgid);","","            /* If foreground and interactive, assign terminal control */","            if (!pipeline->is_background && g_shell.is_interactive) {","                tcsetpgrp(g_shell.shell_terminal, pgid);","            }","","            /* Restore default signal handlers */","            signals_reset_child();","","            /* Wire pipeline input */","            if (prev_pipe_read != -1) {","                if (dup2(prev_pipe_read, STDIN_FILENO) < 0) {","                    forge_perror(\"dup2 pipe in\");","                    _exit(1);","                }","                close(prev_pipe_read);","            }","","            /* Wire pipeline output */","            if (i < num_cmds - 1) {","                close(pipefds[0]);","                if (dup2(pipefds[1], STDOUT_FILENO) < 0) {","                    forge_perror(\"dup2 pipe out\");","                    _exit(1);","                }","                close(pipefds[1]);","            }","","            /* Apply file descriptor redirections */","            if (apply_redirections(cmd) < 0) {","                _exit(1);","            }","","            /* Execute builtin if part of a pipeline */","            if (is_builtin(cmd->argv[0])) {","                int code = execute_builtin(cmd->argc, cmd->argv);","                _exit(code);","            }","","            /* Execute external command */","            execvp(cmd->argv[0], cmd->argv);","","            if (errno == ENOENT) {","                fprintf(stderr, \"forge: %s: command not found\\n\", cmd->argv[0]);","                _exit(127);","            } else if (errno == EACCES) {","                fprintf(stderr, \"forge: %s: Permission denied\\n\", cmd->argv[0]);","                _exit(126);","            } else {","                forge_perror(cmd->argv[0]);","                _exit(126);","            }","        }","","        /* Parent process */","        if (pgid == 0) {","            pgid = pid;","            job->pgid = pgid;","        }","","        /* Setpgid in parent as well to eliminate race conditions */","        setpgid(pid, pgid);","","        /* Add process to job */","        job_add_process(job, pid);","","        if (prev_pipe_read != -1) {","            close(prev_pipe_read);","        }","","        if (i < num_cmds - 1) {","            close(pipefds[1]);","            prev_pipe_read = pipefds[0];","        } else {","            prev_pipe_read = -1;","        }","    }","","    if (pipeline->is_background) {","        return put_job_in_background(job, false);","    } else {","        return put_job_in_foreground(job, false);","    }","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":2,"html":"<p><code>executor.h</code> brings in everything process-related: <code>sys/wait.h</code> for <code>waitpid</code>, <code>fcntl.h</code> for <code>open</code> flags, <code>termios.h</code> for terminal control, and the parser, job and signal headers.</p>"},{"start":3,"end":9,"html":"<p>The shell's global state, in one struct. It holds the last exit status (what Bash calls <code>$?</code>), whether we're interactive, the shell's own process group ID, the terminal's file descriptor, and the saved terminal settings. The designated initializers (<code>.field = value</code>) are C99 syntax. Everything here is reset in <code>shell_init()</code> anyway.</p>"},{"start":10,"end":15,"html":"<p><strong>Interactive or not?</strong> <code>isatty(0)</code> asks whether stdin is a terminal. Job control (process groups, Ctrl+Z, <code>fg</code>) only makes sense with a terminal, so all of the setup below is skipped when commands are piped in, as they are in the test suite.</p>"},{"start":16,"end":20,"html":"<p>A shell started in the background (for example <code>./bin/forge &amp;</code> from Bash) must not grab the terminal. <code>tcgetpgrp()</code> returns the terminal's foreground process group. While that isn't our group, we send <code>SIGTTIN</code> to our own group, which stops us. When the user brings us to the foreground with <code>fg</code>, we resume, loop, and pass the check. This is the textbook pattern from the GNU libc manual's \"Implementing a Shell\" chapter.</p>"},{"start":21,"end":23,"html":"<p>From here on, the shell ignores Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z, <code>SIGTTIN</code> and <code>SIGTTOU</code>, and watches <code>SIGCHLD</code>. The order matters: <code>SIGTTOU</code> has to be ignored <em>before</em> the <code>tcsetpgrp()</code> call a few lines down, because a process that isn't in the foreground group gets <code>SIGTTOU</code> (and stops) when it tries to change the terminal's foreground group, unless it ignores that signal.</p>"},{"start":24,"end":32,"html":"<p>The shell puts itself in its own process group, with its PID as the group ID, so that keyboard signals aimed at a job's group never hit the shell.</p><p>The <code>getpgrp() != pid</code> check fixes a real bug found while recording the demo. When a terminal emulator starts the shell directly, the shell is a <em>session leader</em> and already leads its own group. POSIX says <code>setpgid()</code> on a session leader fails with <code>EPERM</code>, so the old code printed <code>forge: setpgid: Operation not permitted</code> at startup.</p>"},{"start":33,"end":35,"html":"<p>Makes the shell's group the terminal's foreground group. From now on, only the foreground group can read from the terminal, and Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Z are delivered to whichever group is in the foreground.</p>"},{"start":36,"end":39,"html":"<p>Saves the terminal's settings (echo, line editing, special characters). If a program such as <code>vim</code> or <code>less</code> changes them and then crashes or is stopped, the shell restores these saved settings when it takes the terminal back.</p>"},{"start":40,"end":42,"html":"<p>Starts with an empty job table. This runs even when the shell isn't interactive, because background jobs (<code>&amp;</code>) still work without a terminal.</p>"},{"start":43,"end":45,"html":"<p>Applies a command's <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;</code> and <code>2&gt;</code> redirects to the <em>current</em> process. It's called in the forked child just before <code>execvp()</code>, and in the shell itself (temporarily) for builtins.</p>"},{"start":46,"end":60,"html":"<p>The redirect pattern, the same for all three:</p><p>1. <code>open()</code> the file. The kernel returns the lowest free descriptor, say 3. 2. <code>dup2(fd, 0)</code> makes descriptor 0 (stdin) point at the same open file as fd 3. If 0 was open, it's closed first, atomically. 3. <code>close(fd)</code> drops the now-unneeded extra descriptor.</p><p>The program then reads \"stdin\" without knowing it's a file. That's the whole trick behind redirection.</p>"},{"start":61,"end":76,"html":"<p>For output, the flags decide the behaviour. <code>O_WRONLY | O_CREAT</code> writes and creates the file if it's missing. Then <code>O_TRUNC</code> empties an existing file (<code>&gt;</code>), while <code>O_APPEND</code> makes every write go to the current end of the file (<code>&gt;&gt;</code>). <code>0644</code> is the permission for new files (owner read/write, others read), and the process's <code>umask</code> can reduce it further.</p>"},{"start":77,"end":94,"html":"<p>The same pattern onto descriptor 2. <code>2&gt;</code> always truncates: there's no <code>2&gt;&gt;</code>.</p><p>The order of these three blocks only matters when they fail. If the input file is missing, nothing else is redirected.</p>"},{"start":95,"end":100,"html":"<p><strong>Why builtins run inside the shell:</strong> <code>cd</code> has to change <em>the shell's</em> working directory, and <code>export</code> the shell's environment. A forked child would change its own copy and then exit, and the shell would see no change. So a lone builtin runs in-process, without forking.</p><p>But a builtin can still be redirected (<code>pwd &gt; where.txt</code>), and redirecting the shell's own stdout would break the shell. So the real descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are first saved with <code>dup()</code>, which returns new descriptors pointing at the same terminal.</p>"},{"start":101,"end":120,"html":"<p>Run the builtin with the redirects applied, then <code>dup2()</code> the saved copies back over 0, 1 and 2, and close the copies. The shell's own stdout is the terminal again, as if nothing happened.</p><p><code>exit</code> is the exception: it calls <code>exit()</code> inside <code>execute_builtin</code>, so the shell ends before the restore. That's harmless, because the process is going away anyway.</p>"},{"start":121,"end":131,"html":"<p>The core of the shell: given parsed commands, create the processes. First count how many commands are in the pipeline.</p>"},{"start":132,"end":136,"html":"<p>Fast path: a single builtin in the foreground runs in-process (see above). A builtin in a pipeline (<code>help | head</code>) or in the background (<code>cd /tmp &amp;</code>) goes through the fork path instead, so it runs in a child, and <code>cd /tmp &amp;</code> changes nothing in the shell, just as in Bash.</p>"},{"start":137,"end":139,"html":"<p>Every external command is a <em>job</em>, even a plain foreground <code>ls</code>. The job's process group isn't known yet (it will be the first child's PID), so it starts as 0.</p>"},{"start":140,"end":143,"html":"<p><code>prev_pipe_read</code> carries the read end of the previous command's pipe into the next loop iteration. <code>-1</code> means \"no previous pipe\": the first command reads from the shell's stdin (or its <code>&lt;</code> file).</p>"},{"start":144,"end":153,"html":"<p>One iteration per command. Every command except the last gets a fresh pipe <em>before</em> forking: <code>pipefds[1]</code> is the write end (this command's stdout) and <code>pipefds[0]</code> the read end (the next command's stdin). The pipe must be created before <code>fork()</code> so both parent and child inherit it.</p><p>If <code>pipe()</code> fails (for example because the process ran out of descriptors), the function returns. Note that earlier children in the same pipeline are already running and stay in the job table, unwaited. That's a known rough edge.</p>"},{"start":154,"end":167,"html":"<p><code>fork()</code> duplicates the shell. It returns twice: <code>0</code> in the child and the child's PID in the parent. On failure (<code>-1</code>, usually too many processes or out of memory) the descriptors this iteration owns are closed so they don't leak.</p>"},{"start":168,"end":172,"html":"<p><strong>Child side.</strong> The first child's PID becomes the group ID for the whole pipeline. This works because <code>pgid</code> is a copy of the parent's variable at fork time: it's 0 for the first child and already set for the later ones.</p>"},{"start":173,"end":175,"html":"<p><code>setpgid(0, pgid)</code> moves this child into the job's group. The parent makes <em>the same call</em> for this child right after <code>fork()</code>. Whichever runs first wins, and the second call is harmless. Doing it in both places removes a race: the parent may call <code>tcsetpgrp()</code> or <code>kill(-pgid)</code> before the child has been scheduled, and that would fail if the group didn't exist yet.</p>"},{"start":176,"end":180,"html":"<p>A foreground job takes the terminal itself, again duplicated in the parent (<code>put_job_in_foreground</code>) for the same race reason. This only works because <code>SIGTTOU</code> is still ignored here: the child inherited the shell's dispositions and hasn't reset them yet. Swap this with the next step and a child in a pipeline could stop itself on <code>SIGTTOU</code>.</p>"},{"start":181,"end":183,"html":"<p>The shell ignores Ctrl+C, but the program you run must not, otherwise Ctrl+C couldn't stop <code>sleep</code>. Ignored signals stay ignored across <code>exec()</code>, so the child has to reset them to default <em>before</em> exec. Handled signals are reset by <code>exec()</code> automatically, because the handler code no longer exists.</p>"},{"start":184,"end":192,"html":"<p>If there's a previous pipe, its read end becomes this process's stdin. After <code>dup2</code>, descriptor 0 <em>is</em> the pipe, so the original descriptor is closed.</p>"},{"start":193,"end":202,"html":"<p>If this isn't the last command, stdout goes into the new pipe's write end. The child also closes the read end it doesn't use. This closing isn't optional. A reader sees end-of-file only when <em>every</em> write end of a pipe is closed, in every process. One forgotten descriptor and <code>wc</code> waits forever.</p>"},{"start":203,"end":207,"html":"<p>File redirects are applied <em>after</em> the pipe wiring, so they win: in <code>ls &gt; out.txt | wc</code>, <code>ls</code> writes to the file and <code>wc</code> reads an empty pipe. That's also how Bash behaves.</p>"},{"start":208,"end":213,"html":"<p>A builtin inside a pipeline runs in this child and then <code>_exit</code>s with its status. <code>_exit()</code> instead of <code>exit()</code> matters: <code>exit()</code> would flush stdio buffers the child inherited from the shell (text could print twice) and run the shell's <code>atexit</code> handlers inside the child.</p>"},{"start":214,"end":216,"html":"<p><code>execvp()</code> replaces this process's program with the command. The <code>p</code> means it searches <code>PATH</code>, the <code>v</code> means arguments come as an array. On success it never returns, because the code that called it no longer exists. The PID, open descriptors, process group and ignored or default signal settings all carry over.</p>"},{"start":217,"end":228,"html":"<p>Reaching this line means <code>exec</code> failed. The exit codes follow the shell convention: <strong>127</strong> for \"command not found\" and <strong>126</strong> for \"found but can't run\" (no permission, a directory, a bad binary format). The tests check both.</p>"},{"start":229,"end":240,"html":"<p><strong>Parent side.</strong> The parent records the group ID from the first child's PID, makes its half of the double <code>setpgid</code> call, and adds the PID to the job.</p>"},{"start":241,"end":252,"html":"<p>The parent closes its copy of the previous pipe's read end, since only the child that was just forked needs it, and its copy of the new pipe's write end. It keeps the new read end for the next command. After the loop, the shell holds <em>no</em> pipe descriptors. The test suite checks this by counting entries in <code>/proc/self/fd</code> after running several pipelines.</p>"},{"start":253,"end":258,"html":"<p>Every process now exists. A background job prints <code>[1] &lt;pgid&gt;</code> and returns at once, with no waiting. A foreground job hands the terminal over, waits for the job to finish or stop, and takes the terminal back (see <code>src/jobs.c</code>).</p>"}]},{"path":"src/builtins.c","title":"Commands the shell runs itself instead of starting a program: cd, pwd, exit, export, unset, help, plus dispatch.","group":"Shell core","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"builtins.h\"","#include \"jobs.h\"","#include \"executor.h\"","","extern char **environ;","","/* Forward declarations for proc observability builtins (implemented in Milestone 5) */","int builtin_inspect(int argc, char **argv) __attribute__((weak));","int builtin_monitor(int argc, char **argv) __attribute__((weak));","int builtin_ps(int argc, char **argv) __attribute__((weak));","","bool is_builtin(const char *name) {","    if (!name) return false;","    return (strcmp(name, \"cd\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"pwd\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"exit\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"export\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"unset\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"jobs\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"fg\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"bg\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"help\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"inspect\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"monitor\") == 0 ||","            strcmp(name, \"ps\") == 0);","}","","int builtin_cd(int argc, char **argv) {","    const char *target = NULL;","    char cwd_buf[PATH_MAX];","","    if (argc == 1 || strcmp(argv[1], \"~\") == 0) {","        target = getenv(\"HOME\");","        if (!target) {","            forge_error(\"cd: HOME not set\");","            return 1;","        }","    } else if (strcmp(argv[1], \"-\") == 0) {","        target = getenv(\"OLDPWD\");","        if (!target) {","            forge_error(\"cd: OLDPWD not set\");","            return 1;","        }","        printf(\"%s\\n\", target);","    } else {","        target = argv[1];","    }","","    if (!getcwd(cwd_buf, sizeof(cwd_buf))) {","        cwd_buf[0] = '\\0';","    }","","    if (chdir(target) != 0) {","        forge_perror(target);","        return 1;","    }","","    if (cwd_buf[0] != '\\0') {","        setenv(\"OLDPWD\", cwd_buf, 1);","    }","","    char new_cwd[PATH_MAX];","    if (getcwd(new_cwd, sizeof(new_cwd))) {","        setenv(\"PWD\", new_cwd, 1);","    }","","    return 0;","}","","int builtin_pwd(int argc, char **argv) {","    (void)argc;","    (void)argv;","    char cwd_buf[PATH_MAX];","    if (getcwd(cwd_buf, sizeof(cwd_buf))) {","        printf(\"%s\\n\", cwd_buf);","        return 0;","    } else {","        forge_perror(\"pwd\");","        return 1;","    }","}","","int builtin_exit(int argc, char **argv) {","    int code = g_shell.last_exit_status;","    if (argc > 1) {","        code = atoi(argv[1]);","    }","    exit(code);","}","","int builtin_export(int argc, char **argv) {","    if (argc == 1) {","        /* Print all environment variables */","        for (char **env = environ; *env; env++) {","            printf(\"declare -x %s\\n\", *env);","        }","        return 0;","    }","","    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {","        char *eq = strchr(argv[i], '=');","        if (eq) {","            *eq = '\\0';","            const char *key = argv[i];","            const char *val = eq + 1;","            if (setenv(key, val, 1) != 0) {","                forge_perror(\"export\");","                *eq = '=';","                return 1;","            }","            *eq = '=';","        } else {","            /* If no equals sign, keep existing value or export empty */","            const char *val = getenv(argv[i]);","            if (val) {","                setenv(argv[i], val, 1);","            }","        }","    }","    return 0;","}","","int builtin_unset(int argc, char **argv) {","    if (argc < 2) {","        forge_error(\"unset: not enough arguments\");","        return 1;","    }","","    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {","        if (unsetenv(argv[i]) != 0) {","            forge_perror(\"unset\");","            return 1;","        }","    }","    return 0;","}","","int builtin_help(int argc, char **argv) {","    (void)argc;","    (void)argv;","","    printf(\"\\n%sForgeShell v%s - Resource-Aware Unix Shell%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD, FORGE_VERSION, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"Built for systems programming, process management, and Linux observability.\\n\\n\");","    printf(\"%sBuiltin Commands:%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  cd [dir]            Change directory (supports HOME, -, ~)\\n\");","    printf(\"  pwd                 Print current working directory\\n\");","    printf(\"  export KEY=VAL      Set environment variable\\n\");","    printf(\"  unset KEY           Unset environment variable\\n\");","    printf(\"  jobs                List background and stopped jobs\\n\");","    printf(\"  fg [%%id]            Bring job to foreground\\n\");","    printf(\"  bg [%%id]            Resume stopped job in background\\n\");","    printf(\"  inspect <pid>       Deep Linux /proc inspection of process\\n\");","    printf(\"  monitor <pid> [sec] Live CPU and RSS memory monitor\\n\");","    printf(\"  ps                  Process status overview\\n\");","    printf(\"  help                Display this reference guide\\n\");","    printf(\"  exit [code]         Exit ForgeShell\\n\\n\");","    printf(\"%sJob Control & Operators:%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  cmd &               Execute command in background\\n\");","    printf(\"  cmd1 | cmd2         Pipeline stdout to stdin\\n\");","    printf(\"  cmd < in > out      File descriptor redirection\\n\");","    printf(\"  cmd >> out 2> err   Append stdout and redirect stderr\\n\");","    printf(\"  Ctrl+C              Interrupt foreground process group\\n\");","    printf(\"  Ctrl+Z              Suspend foreground process group\\n\\n\");","    return 0;","}","","int execute_builtin(int argc, char **argv) {","    if (argc <= 0 || !argv[0]) return 0;","    const char *name = argv[0];","","    if (strcmp(name, \"cd\") == 0) return builtin_cd(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"pwd\") == 0) return builtin_pwd(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"exit\") == 0) return builtin_exit(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"export\") == 0) return builtin_export(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"unset\") == 0) return builtin_unset(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"jobs\") == 0) return builtin_jobs(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"fg\") == 0) return builtin_fg(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"bg\") == 0) return builtin_bg(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"help\") == 0) return builtin_help(argc, argv);","","    if (strcmp(name, \"inspect\") == 0 && builtin_inspect) return builtin_inspect(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"monitor\") == 0 && builtin_monitor) return builtin_monitor(argc, argv);","    if (strcmp(name, \"ps\") == 0 && builtin_ps) return builtin_ps(argc, argv);","","    forge_error(\"%s: builtin not yet implemented\", name);","    return 1;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p><code>environ</code> is the process's environment, a <code>NULL</code>-terminated array of <code>\"KEY=value\"</code> strings that libc maintains. Child processes inherit it through <code>execvp()</code>, which is how <code>export</code> reaches programs you run later.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":11,"html":"<p>These are <strong>weak symbols</strong>, left over from building the project in stages. A weak declaration lets the program link even when no definition exists, and the function pointer is then <code>NULL</code>. That's why <code>execute_builtin</code> checks <code>&amp;&amp; builtin_inspect</code> before calling it. Now that <code>src/proc.c</code> always provides them, the weak attribute changes nothing. It's a nice thing to be able to explain if someone asks.</p>"},{"start":12,"end":27,"html":"<p>A plain list of names. The executor asks this before forking. A hash table would be overkill for twelve names: <code>strcmp</code> against a dozen short strings costs nothing next to a <code>fork()</code>.</p>"},{"start":28,"end":48,"html":"<p><code>cd</code> must be a builtin: the working directory belongs to a process, and a child can't change its parent's.</p><p>Three forms: <code>cd</code> or <code>cd ~</code> goes to <code>$HOME</code>, <code>cd -</code> goes to <code>$OLDPWD</code> (and prints it, as Bash does), and anything else is a path. Note that only a bare <code>~</code> is expanded: <code>cd ~/src</code> looks for a directory literally named <code>~</code>.</p>"},{"start":49,"end":52,"html":"<p>The current directory is recorded <em>before</em> moving, so it can become <code>OLDPWD</code>. <code>getcwd</code> can fail (for example if the current directory was deleted), in which case <code>OLDPWD</code> is simply left as it was.</p>"},{"start":53,"end":57,"html":"<p><code>chdir()</code> is the actual system call. On failure, <code>forge_perror</code> prints the reason from <code>errno</code>, such as \"No such file or directory\" or \"Permission denied\".</p>"},{"start":58,"end":69,"html":"<p>After a successful move, <code>OLDPWD</code> and <code>PWD</code> are updated in the environment, so child processes (and <code>cd -</code>) see them. <code>setenv(..., 1)</code> overwrites an existing value.</p>"},{"start":70,"end":82,"html":"<p>Asks the kernel for the real current directory with <code>getcwd()</code>, rather than trusting <code>$PWD</code>, which anyone can change with <code>export</code>.</p>"},{"start":83,"end":90,"html":"<p><code>exit</code> with no argument reuses the last command's status, so a script that ends with <code>exit</code> reports how its last command went. <code>exit 42</code> exits with 42. <code>atoi</code> turns junk like <code>exit abc</code> into 0, and values over 255 wrap around, because the kernel keeps only the low 8 bits of an exit status. A real shell would also warn about stopped jobs before exiting. This one doesn't.</p>"},{"start":91,"end":99,"html":"<p>With no arguments, <code>export</code> lists the environment in the same <code>declare -x</code> format Bash uses.</p>"},{"start":100,"end":111,"html":"<p><code>export KEY=value</code> splits on the first <code>=</code> by temporarily writing a <code>'\\0'</code> over it, so <code>argv[i]</code> reads as just the key, without copying. The <code>=</code> is put back afterwards on every path, success or failure, because <code>argv</code> still belongs to the parsed command and will be freed later.</p><p><code>setenv</code> copies both strings, so nothing points into <code>argv</code> once the command is freed. Exported variables reach later child processes because <code>execvp</code> passes <code>environ</code> along.</p>"},{"start":112,"end":122,"html":"<p><code>export KEY</code> without a value re-exports an existing variable. In this shell every variable is already in the environment, so this effectively does nothing. It exists so scripts written for Bash don't fail.</p>"},{"start":123,"end":137,"html":"<p>Removes each named variable from the environment. <code>unsetenv</code> only fails for invalid names (empty, or containing <code>=</code>).</p>"},{"start":138,"end":166,"html":"<p>Prints the built-in reference. <code>%%</code> in a <code>printf</code> format prints a literal <code>%</code>, which is how <code>fg [%id]</code> shows up correctly.</p>"},{"start":167,"end":187,"html":"<p>Dispatch by name. It returns the builtin's exit status, which the executor stores as the shell's last status. The <code>/proc</code> builtins are called through the weak-symbol check described at the top of the file. The final error can't actually be reached today, because every name <code>is_builtin</code> accepts is handled here.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/jobs.c","title":"The job table and job control: waiting, stopping, resuming, and moving jobs between foreground and background.","group":"Job control","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"jobs.h\"","#include \"executor.h\"","#include <sys/wait.h>","#include <ctype.h>","","static Job *g_job_list = NULL;","","void jobs_init(void) {","    g_job_list = NULL;","}","","/* Job IDs work like Bash: one more than the highest ID still in the table,"," * so numbering restarts at 1 once every job has finished. */","static int next_job_id(void) {","    int max_id = 0;","    for (Job *j = g_job_list; j; j = j->next) {","        if (j->id > max_id) {","            max_id = j->id;","        }","    }","    return max_id + 1;","}","","Job *job_create(pid_t pgid, const char *cmd_str) {","    Job *job = xmalloc(sizeof(Job));","    job->id = next_job_id();","    job->pgid = pgid;","    job->state = JOB_STATE_RUNNING;","    job->command_str = xstrdup(cmd_str);","    job->first_process = NULL;","    job->notified = false;","    job->next = NULL;","","    /* Append to job list */","    if (!g_job_list) {","        g_job_list = job;","    } else {","        Job *curr = g_job_list;","        while (curr->next) {","            curr = curr->next;","        }","        curr->next = job;","    }","","    return job;","}","","void job_add_process(Job *job, pid_t pid) {","    if (!job) return;","    Process *proc = xmalloc(sizeof(Process));","    proc->pid = pid;","    proc->state = JOB_STATE_RUNNING;","    proc->status = 0;","    proc->completed = false;","    proc->stopped = false;","    proc->next = NULL;","","    if (!job->first_process) {","        job->first_process = proc;","    } else {","        Process *curr = job->first_process;","        while (curr->next) {","            curr = curr->next;","        }","        curr->next = proc;","    }","}","","Job *job_find_by_id(int id) {","    Job *curr = g_job_list;","    while (curr) {","        if (curr->id == id) {","            return curr;","        }","        curr = curr->next;","    }","    return NULL;","}","","Job *job_find_by_pgid(pid_t pgid) {","    Job *curr = g_job_list;","    while (curr) {","        if (curr->pgid == pgid) {","            return curr;","        }","        curr = curr->next;","    }","    return NULL;","}","","Job *job_get_most_recent(void) {","    if (!g_job_list) return NULL;","    Job *curr = g_job_list;","    while (curr->next) {","        curr = curr->next;","    }","    return curr;","}","","void job_free(Job *job) {","    if (!job) return;","    Process *p = job->first_process;","    while (p) {","        Process *next = p->next;","        free(p);","        p = next;","    }","    if (job->command_str) {","        free(job->command_str);","    }","    free(job);","}","","void job_remove(Job *job) {","    if (!job || !g_job_list) return;","    if (g_job_list == job) {","        g_job_list = job->next;","        job_free(job);","        return;","    }","    Job *prev = g_job_list;","    while (prev->next && prev->next != job) {","        prev = prev->next;","    }","    if (prev->next == job) {","        prev->next = job->next;","        job_free(job);","    }","}","","bool job_is_completed(const Job *job) {","    if (!job || !job->first_process) return true;","    for (Process *p = job->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","        if (!p->completed) {","            return false;","        }","    }","    return true;","}","","bool job_is_stopped(const Job *job) {","    if (!job || !job->first_process) return false;","    bool has_stopped = false;","    for (Process *p = job->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","        if (!p->completed) {","            if (!p->stopped) {","                return false; /* At least one process is still running */","            }","            has_stopped = true;","        }","    }","    return has_stopped;","}","","static void update_process_status(pid_t pid, int status) {","    for (Job *j = g_job_list; j; j = j->next) {","        for (Process *p = j->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","            if (p->pid == pid) {","                p->status = status;","                if (WIFSTOPPED(status)) {","                    p->stopped = true;","                    p->state = JOB_STATE_STOPPED;","                } else if (WIFCONTINUED(status)) {","                    p->stopped = false;","                    p->state = JOB_STATE_RUNNING;","                } else {","                    p->completed = true;","                    p->stopped = false;","                    p->state = JOB_STATE_DONE;","                }","                return;","            }","        }","    }","}","","int wait_for_job(Job *job) {","    if (!job) return 0;","","    int last_status = 0;","    while (!job_is_stopped(job) && !job_is_completed(job)) {","        int status = 0;","        pid_t pid = waitpid(-job->pgid, &status, WUNTRACED);","        if (pid < 0) {","            if (errno == ECHILD) {","                /* All children reaped */","                for (Process *p = job->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","                    p->completed = true;","                }","                break;","            }","            if (errno == EINTR) {","                continue;","            }","            forge_perror(\"waitpid\");","            break;","        }","","        update_process_status(pid, status);","        last_status = status;","    }","","    if (job_is_completed(job)) {","        job->state = JOB_STATE_DONE;","        /* Like Bash, a pipeline's status is the status of its last command,","         * not of whichever process happened to be reaped last. */","        Process *last = job->first_process;","        while (last && last->next) {","            last = last->next;","        }","        if (last) {","            last_status = last->status;","        }","        if (WIFEXITED(last_status)) {","            g_shell.last_exit_status = WEXITSTATUS(last_status);","        } else if (WIFSIGNALED(last_status)) {","            int sig = WTERMSIG(last_status);","            g_shell.last_exit_status = 128 + sig;","            if (sig == SIGINT) {","                printf(\"\\n\");","            } else if (sig == SIGSEGV) {","                fprintf(stderr, \"forge: Segmentation fault (core dumped)\\n\");","            } else if (sig == SIGABRT) {","                fprintf(stderr, \"forge: Aborted (core dumped)\\n\");","            }","        }","    } else if (job_is_stopped(job)) {","        job->state = JOB_STATE_STOPPED;","        job->notified = true; /* reported here, so check_and_notify_jobs() stays quiet */","        printf(\"\\n[%d]+ Stopped                 %s\\n\", job->id, job->command_str);","        g_shell.last_exit_status = 128 + SIGTSTP;","    }","","    return g_shell.last_exit_status;","}","","int put_job_in_foreground(Job *job, bool cont) {","    if (!job) return 1;","","    /* Transfer terminal control to the job's process group */","    if (g_shell.is_interactive) {","        if (tcsetpgrp(g_shell.shell_terminal, job->pgid) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"tcsetpgrp\");","        }","    }","","    /* If reviving a stopped job, send SIGCONT */","    if (cont) {","        job->state = JOB_STATE_RUNNING;","        job->notified = false;","        for (Process *p = job->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","            p->stopped = false;","        }","        if (kill(-job->pgid, SIGCONT) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"kill (SIGCONT)\");","        }","        /* Print command name when brought to foreground via fg */","        printf(\"%s\\n\", job->command_str);","        fflush(stdout);","    }","","    /* Wait for the job to complete or be suspended */","    int status = wait_for_job(job);","","    /* Reclaim terminal control for the shell */","    if (g_shell.is_interactive) {","        if (tcsetpgrp(g_shell.shell_terminal, g_shell.shell_pgid) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"tcsetpgrp restore\");","        }","        tcsetattr(g_shell.shell_terminal, TCSADRAIN, &g_shell.shell_tmodes);","    }","","    /* If completed, delete from job list */","    if (job_is_completed(job)) {","        job_remove(job);","    }","","    return status;","}","","int put_job_in_background(Job *job, bool cont) {","    if (!job) return 1;","","    job->state = JOB_STATE_RUNNING;","    if (cont) {","        job->notified = false;","        for (Process *p = job->first_process; p; p = p->next) {","            p->stopped = false;","        }","        if (kill(-job->pgid, SIGCONT) < 0) {","            forge_perror(\"kill (SIGCONT)\");","        }","        printf(\"[%d]+ %s &\\n\", job->id, job->command_str);","    } else {","        printf(\"[%d] %d\\n\", job->id, (int)job->pgid);","    }","    return 0;","}","","void check_and_notify_jobs(void) {","    int status;","    pid_t pid;","","    /* Non-blocking check for any state changes in child processes */","    while ((pid = waitpid(-1, &status, WNOHANG | WUNTRACED | WCONTINUED)) > 0) {","        update_process_status(pid, status);","    }","","    /* Scan job list and notify completed / stopped background jobs */","    Job *curr = g_job_list;","    while (curr) {","        Job *next = curr->next;","        if (job_is_completed(curr)) {","            printf(\"[%d]+ Done                    %s\\n\", curr->id, curr->command_str);","            job_remove(curr);","        } else if (job_is_stopped(curr) && !curr->notified) {","            printf(\"[%d]+ Stopped                 %s\\n\", curr->id, curr->command_str);","            curr->notified = true;","        }","        curr = next;","    }","}","","int builtin_jobs(int argc, char **argv) {","    (void)argc;","    (void)argv;","","    check_and_notify_jobs();","","    if (!g_job_list) {","        return 0;","    }","","    Job *recent = job_get_most_recent();","    for (Job *j = g_job_list; j; j = j->next) {","        char mark = (j == recent) ? '+' : '-';","        const char *state_str = \"Running\";","        if (j->state == JOB_STATE_STOPPED) {","            state_str = \"Stopped\";","        } else if (j->state == JOB_STATE_DONE) {","            state_str = \"Done\";","        }","        printf(\"[%d]%c %-10s %s\\n\", j->id, mark, state_str, j->command_str);","    }","","    return 0;","}","","static Job *parse_job_spec(const char *arg) {","    if (!arg) {","        return job_get_most_recent();","    }","    if (arg[0] == '%') {","        arg++;","    }","    int id = atoi(arg);","    if (id <= 0) {","        return NULL;","    }","    return job_find_by_id(id);","}","","int builtin_fg(int argc, char **argv) {","    const char *spec = (argc > 1) ? argv[1] : NULL;","    Job *job = parse_job_spec(spec);","    if (!job) {","        if (spec) {","            forge_error(\"fg: %s: no such job\", spec);","        } else {","            forge_error(\"fg: current: no such job\");","        }","        return 1;","    }","","    return put_job_in_foreground(job, true);","}","","int builtin_bg(int argc, char **argv) {","    const char *spec = (argc > 1) ? argv[1] : NULL;","    Job *job = parse_job_spec(spec);","    if (!job) {","        if (spec) {","            forge_error(\"bg: %s: no such job\", spec);","        } else {","            forge_error(\"bg: current: no such job\");","        }","        return 1;","    }","","    return put_job_in_background(job, true);","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":5,"html":"<p>Includes the executor header for <code>g_shell</code> (the terminal and the shell's group ID) and <code>sys/wait.h</code> for the <code>W*</code> status macros.</p>"},{"start":6,"end":11,"html":"<p>The job table is a singly linked list, kept in creation order. <code>static</code> makes it private to this file, so other code can only reach it through the functions below.</p>"},{"start":12,"end":23,"html":"<p><strong>A bug fixed while making the demo.</strong> Job IDs used to come from a counter that only went up, and every foreground command also used a number, so after running <code>ls</code> your first background job showed as <code>[2]</code>. Now an ID is one more than the highest ID still in the table. Numbers restart at <code>[1]</code> once the table is empty, which matches Bash. The new test \"Job numbering starts at [1]\" covers it.</p>"},{"start":24,"end":47,"html":"<p>A job starts out running, with no processes and not yet reported. It's appended at the tail, so <code>jobs</code> lists in creation order and the last job is the \"current\" one (<code>+</code>). Walking to the tail is O(n), which is fine for a table that rarely holds more than a few jobs.</p>"},{"start":48,"end":68,"html":"<p>A job is one process per pipeline stage. <code>ls | sort | head</code> is one job with three <code>Process</code> entries, all in one process group. Each process tracks its own <code>completed</code> and <code>stopped</code> flags, because they change one at a time as <code>waitpid</code> reports them.</p>"},{"start":69,"end":79,"html":"<p>Linear search by job number, used by <code>fg 2</code> and <code>bg %2</code>.</p>"},{"start":80,"end":90,"html":"<p>Linear search by process group. Declared for completeness, but nothing calls it at the moment.</p>"},{"start":91,"end":99,"html":"<p>The tail of the list: the job <code>fg</code> and <code>bg</code> use when you don't give a number, and the one marked <code>+</code> in <code>jobs</code>.</p>"},{"start":100,"end":113,"html":"<p>Frees the process list, the saved command string and the job itself. Only <code>job_remove</code> calls it, after unlinking the job.</p>"},{"start":114,"end":130,"html":"<p>Standard linked-list removal: if the job is the head, move the head forward. Otherwise find the node <em>before</em> it and link around it. The <code>prev-&gt;next == job</code> check makes removing a job that isn't in the list a safe no-op.</p>"},{"start":131,"end":140,"html":"<p>A job is done only when <strong>every</strong> process in it is done. In <code>yes | head -n 1</code>, <code>head</code> exits first, and <code>yes</code> dies a moment later from <code>SIGPIPE</code> when it writes to a pipe nobody reads.</p>"},{"start":141,"end":154,"html":"<p>A job counts as stopped when every process that hasn't finished is stopped and at least one is stopped. Ctrl+Z stops the whole group at once, but <code>waitpid</code> reports each process separately, so this check is what decides that the job as a whole is stopped.</p>"},{"start":155,"end":176,"html":"<p>Takes a PID and the status word from <code>waitpid</code> and updates the matching <code>Process</code>. The status macros decode the kernel's encoding:</p><p><code>WIFSTOPPED</code>: stopped by a signal (Ctrl+Z, <code>SIGTTIN</code>). <code>WIFCONTINUED</code>: resumed with <code>SIGCONT</code>. Anything else is a real end: a normal exit (<code>WIFEXITED</code>) or death by a signal (<code>WIFSIGNALED</code>). Either way, the process is completed and the kernel has freed its zombie.</p>"},{"start":177,"end":184,"html":"<p>Blocks until the foreground job finishes or stops. <code>waitpid(-pgid, ...)</code> with a <em>negative</em> PID means \"any child in process group <code>pgid</code>\". <code>WUNTRACED</code> also returns when a child <em>stops</em>. Without it, Ctrl+Z would leave the shell waiting forever on a frozen job.</p>"},{"start":185,"end":198,"html":"<p><code>ECHILD</code> means there are no children left in that group to wait for (something else already reaped them). The job is marked completed so the loop can't spin. <code>EINTR</code> means a signal handler interrupted the wait (<code>SIGCHLD</code> itself can do that), so the wait simply restarts.</p>"},{"start":199,"end":202,"html":"<p>Each reaped process updates the table, and <code>last_status</code> remembers the most recent status as a fallback.</p>"},{"start":203,"end":226,"html":"<p>Turns the raw status into the shell's exit status.</p><p><strong>Which status?</strong> Like Bash, the status of a pipeline is the status of its <em>last command</em>, the tail of the process list. That's not necessarily the last process to <em>finish</em>. This was a bug fixed today: the code used whichever process was reaped last, so <code>sleep 1 | false</code> reported 0 (success) because <code>sleep</code> finished after <code>false</code>. Bash says 1. The test \"Pipeline exit status (last command)\" now checks both orders.</p><p>A normal exit gives the code passed to <code>exit()</code>. Death by signal gives <strong>128 + signal number</strong>, the convention every shell uses: Ctrl+C gives 130 and a segfault 139. The test suite checks for 139 with <code>kill -SEGV $$</code>.</p>"},{"start":227,"end":236,"html":"<p>The job was stopped (usually Ctrl+Z). Print the notice, record status 148 (128 + <code>SIGTSTP</code>), and leave the job in the table so <code>fg</code> or <code>bg</code> can resume it.</p><p><code>notified = true</code> is another fix from today: without it, <code>check_and_notify_jobs()</code> saw the same stopped job before the next prompt and printed \"Stopped\" a second time.</p>"},{"start":237,"end":246,"html":"<p>The foreground dance:</p><p>1. Give the terminal to the job's group with <code>tcsetpgrp</code>. Now Ctrl+C and Ctrl+Z go to the job, not the shell. 2. If resuming (<code>fg</code>), mark everything running and send <code>SIGCONT</code> to the whole group (<code>kill</code> with a negative PID targets a group). 3. Wait. 4. Take the terminal back and restore the saved terminal settings.</p>"},{"start":247,"end":261,"html":"<p><code>notified</code> is reset here so that if the job is stopped again later, that stop gets reported too. <code>fg</code> echoes the command line, as Bash does.</p>"},{"start":262,"end":264,"html":"<p>The shell blocks here while the job runs. This is the moment the shell \"waits\" for your command.</p>"},{"start":265,"end":272,"html":"<p>The shell takes the terminal back whether the job finished or was stopped. <code>tcsetattr(..., TCSADRAIN, ...)</code> restores the terminal settings saved at startup, once pending output has been written. That's how the shell recovers if a full-screen program such as <code>less</code> or <code>vim</code> is stopped while the terminal is in raw mode.</p>"},{"start":273,"end":280,"html":"<p>A finished foreground job is removed straight away, so it never shows up as \"Done\". A stopped one stays in the table.</p>"},{"start":281,"end":299,"html":"<p>Two cases. A new background job (<code>cmd &amp;</code>) just prints <code>[id] pgid</code> and returns: the shell never waits for it, and it's reaped later by <code>check_and_notify_jobs</code>. A <code>bg</code> on a stopped job sends <code>SIGCONT</code> to its group so it runs again <em>without</em> the terminal.</p><p>If a background job tries to read from the terminal, the kernel sends it <code>SIGTTIN</code>, which stops it, and <code>jobs</code> will show it as stopped.</p>"},{"start":300,"end":308,"html":"<p>Runs before every prompt. <code>waitpid(-1, ..., WNOHANG | WUNTRACED | WCONTINUED)</code> asks: \"has <em>any</em> child changed state? Don't block if not.\" The loop drains every pending change. This is what stops background jobs from piling up as <strong>zombies</strong>: a zombie is a finished process whose exit status nobody has collected yet.</p><p>The <code>SIGCHLD</code> handler in <code>signals.c</code> sets a flag that nothing reads. All reaping happens here, at a safe point in the main loop, rather than inside the signal handler. The design is deliberate: almost nothing is safe to call from a signal handler.</p>"},{"start":309,"end":323,"html":"<p>Then the table is scanned. Finished jobs print <code>Done</code> and are removed, and stopped jobs print <code>Stopped</code> once (the <code>notified</code> flag). <code>next</code> is saved before <code>job_remove</code> frees <code>curr</code>, the same use-after-free precaution as in <code>free_pipeline</code>.</p>"},{"start":324,"end":348,"html":"<p><code>jobs</code> first reaps (so finished jobs show as Done and disappear), then prints each job with <code>+</code> for the most recent and <code>-</code> for the rest. The format <code>[1]+ Running    sleep 20 &amp;</code> matches what you'd expect from Bash.</p>"},{"start":349,"end":362,"html":"<p>Turns <code>fg</code>'s argument into a job. No argument means the most recent job, and <code>%2</code> and <code>2</code> both mean job 2. <code>atoi</code> returns 0 for junk, which is rejected.</p>"},{"start":363,"end":377,"html":"<p><code>fg</code> brings a job to the foreground and resumes it (<code>cont = true</code>), then the shell waits for it. Error messages match Bash's, so <code>fg</code> with no jobs says <code>fg: current: no such job</code>.</p>"},{"start":378,"end":391,"html":"<p><code>bg</code> resumes a stopped job in the background. Running <code>bg</code> on a job that's already running is harmless: <code>SIGCONT</code> to a running process does nothing.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/signals.c","title":"How the shell and its children treat signals: Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z, terminal access and child state changes.","group":"Job control","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"signals.h\"","","volatile sig_atomic_t g_sigchld_flag = 0;","","static void handle_sigchld(int sig) {","    (void)sig;","    /* Signal handler performs only async-signal-safe flag setting */","    g_sigchld_flag = 1;","}","","void signals_init_shell(void) {","    struct sigaction sa_chld;","    memset(&sa_chld, 0, sizeof(sa_chld));","    sa_chld.sa_handler = handle_sigchld;","    sa_chld.sa_flags = SA_RESTART | SA_NOCLDSTOP;","    sigemptyset(&sa_chld.sa_mask);","    if (sigaction(SIGCHLD, &sa_chld, NULL) < 0) {","        forge_perror(\"sigaction SIGCHLD\");","    }","","    /* Ignore interactive job control and terminal signals in the shell */","    signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN);","    signal(SIGTSTP, SIG_IGN);","    signal(SIGQUIT, SIG_IGN);","    signal(SIGTTIN, SIG_IGN);","    signal(SIGTTOU, SIG_IGN);","}","","void signals_reset_child(void) {","    /* Restore standard POSIX signal handlers in child process */","    signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL);","    signal(SIGTSTP, SIG_DFL);","    signal(SIGQUIT, SIG_DFL);","    signal(SIGTTIN, SIG_DFL);","    signal(SIGTTOU, SIG_DFL);","    signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL);","","    /* Unblock all signals */","    sigset_t mask;","    sigemptyset(&mask);","    sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &mask, NULL);","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":4,"html":"<p><code>volatile sig_atomic_t</code> is the only type the C standard guarantees a signal handler can safely write and the main program safely read. <code>sig_atomic_t</code> is read and written in one indivisible step, and <code>volatile</code> stops the compiler from keeping the value in a register where the handler's write would never be seen.</p>"},{"start":5,"end":10,"html":"<p>The <code>SIGCHLD</code> handler does the one thing that is always safe: set a flag. A signal can arrive in the middle of <em>any</em> instruction, including halfway through a <code>malloc</code> or <code>printf</code> in the main program. Calling <code>printf</code>, <code>malloc</code> or the job-table code from here could corrupt their internal state. Only <em>async-signal-safe</em> functions (<code>write</code>, <code>_exit</code>, <code>waitpid</code>, ...) may be called from a handler.</p><p>Honest note: nothing ever reads <code>g_sigchld_flag</code>. Reaping happens before every prompt in <code>check_and_notify_jobs()</code>. The handler still has two uses: it shows the safe pattern, and installing it makes sure <code>SIGCHLD</code> isn't ignored. If <code>SIGCHLD</code> were set to <code>SIG_IGN</code>, Linux would reap children automatically and <code>waitpid</code> could never see their exit statuses.</p>"},{"start":11,"end":20,"html":"<p><code>sigaction</code> is the reliable, modern way to install a handler. The old <code>signal()</code> has historically varied between systems.</p><p><code>SA_RESTART</code>: if the signal interrupts a slow system call such as <code>read()</code> inside <code>getline()</code>, the kernel restarts that call instead of failing it with <code>EINTR</code>. Without it, every background job finishing would make the prompt's <code>getline()</code> return an error.</p><p><code>SA_NOCLDSTOP</code>: don't send <code>SIGCHLD</code> when a child merely stops. Stops are still found by polling with <code>WUNTRACED</code>.</p>"},{"start":21,"end":28,"html":"<p>The interactive shell must survive the keys meant for its jobs:</p><p><code>SIGINT</code> (Ctrl+C) and <code>SIGQUIT</code> (Ctrl+\\\\) must not kill the shell. <code>SIGTSTP</code> (Ctrl+Z) must not stop the shell. <code>SIGTTIN</code> and <code>SIGTTOU</code> are what the kernel sends to a <em>background</em> group that reads from, or takes control of, the terminal. Ignoring <code>SIGTTOU</code> is what lets the shell call <code>tcsetpgrp()</code> while it isn't the foreground group.</p><p>In practice the keyboard signals are only sent to the <em>foreground</em> group, which is the job's group while a job runs. Ignoring them is the safety net for the moments when the shell itself is in the foreground.</p>"},{"start":29,"end":37,"html":"<p>Runs in each forked child just before <code>exec</code>. <strong>Ignored signals stay ignored across <code>exec</code></strong>, so without this reset, <code>sleep 100</code> would also ignore Ctrl+C and the user couldn't stop it. Setting <code>SIG_DFL</code> restores the normal behaviour: Ctrl+C terminates, Ctrl+Z stops.</p><p><code>SIGCHLD</code> is reset too, so the new program starts with the default instead of a handler address that won't exist after <code>exec</code>. (<code>exec</code> would reset handled signals anyway. Being explicit costs nothing.)</p>"},{"start":38,"end":42,"html":"<p>The <em>signal mask</em> (the set of blocked signals) is also inherited across <code>fork</code> and <code>exec</code>. The shell doesn't block anything today, but clearing the mask means a future change that blocks <code>SIGCHLD</code> around a critical section can't leave child programs with signals they never receive.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/proc.c","title":"Reads live process data straight from Linux's /proc filesystem: inspect, monitor and ps.","group":"Observability","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"proc.h\"","#include <dirent.h>","#include <time.h>","#include <signal.h>","","static volatile sig_atomic_t g_monitor_interrupted = 0;","","static void monitor_sigint_handler(int sig) {","    (void)sig;","    g_monitor_interrupted = 1;","}","","const char *proc_state_description(char state) {","    switch (state) {","        case 'R': return \"Running (R)\";","        case 'S': return \"Sleeping in interruptible wait (S)\";","        case 'D': return \"Waiting in uninterruptible disk sleep (D)\";","        case 'Z': return \"Zombie (Z)\";","        case 'T': return \"Stopped by job control signal (T)\";","        case 't': return \"Tracing stop / debugger (t)\";","        case 'X': return \"Dead (X)\";","        case 'I': return \"Idle kernel thread (I)\";","        default:  return \"Unknown\";","    }","}","","static double get_system_uptime(void) {","    FILE *fp = fopen(\"/proc/uptime\", \"r\");","    if (!fp) return 0.0;","    double uptime = 0.0;","    if (fscanf(fp, \"%lf\", &uptime) != 1) {","        uptime = 0.0;","    }","    fclose(fp);","    return uptime;","}","","void proc_free_info(ProcInfo *info) {","    if (!info) return;","    if (info->cmdline) {","        free(info->cmdline);","        info->cmdline = NULL;","    }","}","","bool proc_get_info(pid_t pid, ProcInfo *info) {","    if (!info) return false;","    memset(info, 0, sizeof(ProcInfo));","    info->pid = pid;","","    char path[PATH_MAX];","","    /* 1. Parse /proc/<pid>/stat */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/stat\", (int)pid);","    FILE *fp_stat = fopen(path, \"r\");","    if (!fp_stat) {","        return false; /* Process does not exist or terminated */","    }","","    char stat_buf[2048];","    if (!fgets(stat_buf, sizeof(stat_buf), fp_stat)) {","        fclose(fp_stat);","        return false;","    }","    fclose(fp_stat);","","    /* Locate comm between first '(' and last ')' */","    char *open_paren = strchr(stat_buf, '(');","    char *close_paren = strrchr(stat_buf, ')');","    if (!open_paren || !close_paren || close_paren < open_paren) {","        return false;","    }","","    size_t comm_len = close_paren - open_paren - 1;","    if (comm_len >= sizeof(info->comm)) comm_len = sizeof(info->comm) - 1;","    strncpy(info->comm, open_paren + 1, comm_len);","    info->comm[comm_len] = '\\0';","","    /* Parse fields after ')' */","    char state = ' ';","    int ppid = 0, pgrp = 0, session = 0;","    unsigned long utime = 0, stime = 0;","    long num_threads = 1;","    unsigned long long starttime = 0;","","    if (sscanf(close_paren + 2,","               \"%c %d %d %d %*d %*d %*u %*u %*u %*u %*u %lu %lu %*d %*d %*d %*d %ld %*d %llu\",","               &state, &ppid, &pgrp, &session,","               &utime, &stime, &num_threads, &starttime) < 8) {","        return false;","    }","","    info->state = state;","    info->ppid = (pid_t)ppid;","    info->pgrp = (pid_t)pgrp;","    info->session = (pid_t)session;","    info->utime_ticks = utime;","    info->stime_ticks = stime;","    info->num_threads = num_threads;","    info->starttime_ticks = starttime;","    info->state_desc = proc_state_description(state);","","    long clk_tck = sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK);","    if (clk_tck <= 0) clk_tck = 100;","","    info->total_cpu_sec = (double)(utime + stime) / (double)clk_tck;","    double uptime = get_system_uptime();","    double start_sec = (double)starttime / (double)clk_tck;","    info->process_runtime_sec = (uptime > start_sec) ? (uptime - start_sec) : 0.0;","","    /* 2. Parse /proc/<pid>/status */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/status\", (int)pid);","    FILE *fp_status = fopen(path, \"r\");","    if (fp_status) {","        char line[256];","        while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp_status)) {","            if (strncmp(line, \"VmSize:\", 7) == 0) {","                sscanf(line + 7, \" %lu\", &info->vm_size_kb);","            } else if (strncmp(line, \"VmRSS:\", 6) == 0) {","                sscanf(line + 6, \" %lu\", &info->vm_rss_kb);","            } else if (strncmp(line, \"VmData:\", 7) == 0) {","                sscanf(line + 7, \" %lu\", &info->vm_data_kb);","            } else if (strncmp(line, \"VmStk:\", 6) == 0) {","                sscanf(line + 6, \" %lu\", &info->vm_stk_kb);","            } else if (strncmp(line, \"VmExe:\", 6) == 0) {","                sscanf(line + 6, \" %lu\", &info->vm_exe_kb);","            } else if (strncmp(line, \"Threads:\", 8) == 0) {","                long threads = 1;","                if (sscanf(line + 8, \" %ld\", &threads) == 1 && threads > 0) {","                    info->num_threads = threads;","                }","            }","        }","        fclose(fp_status);","    }","","    /* 3. Parse /proc/<pid>/cmdline */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/cmdline\", (int)pid);","    FILE *fp_cmd = fopen(path, \"rb\");","    if (fp_cmd) {","        char cmd_buf[4096];","        size_t n = fread(cmd_buf, 1, sizeof(cmd_buf) - 1, fp_cmd);","        fclose(fp_cmd);","        if (n > 0) {","            for (size_t i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {","                if (cmd_buf[i] == '\\0') {","                    cmd_buf[i] = ' ';","                }","            }","            cmd_buf[n] = '\\0';","            info->cmdline = xstrdup(cmd_buf);","        } else {","            info->cmdline = xstrdup(info->comm);","        }","    } else {","        info->cmdline = xstrdup(info->comm);","    }","","    /* 4. Resolve /proc/<pid>/exe link */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/exe\", (int)pid);","    ssize_t exe_len = readlink(path, info->exe_path, sizeof(info->exe_path) - 1);","    if (exe_len > 0) {","        info->exe_path[exe_len] = '\\0';","    } else {","        strncpy(info->exe_path, \"[kernel thread or unreadable]\", sizeof(info->exe_path) - 1);","        info->exe_path[sizeof(info->exe_path) - 1] = '\\0';","    }","","    /* 5. Resolve /proc/<pid>/cwd link */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/cwd\", (int)pid);","    ssize_t cwd_len = readlink(path, info->cwd_path, sizeof(info->cwd_path) - 1);","    if (cwd_len > 0) {","        info->cwd_path[cwd_len] = '\\0';","    } else {","        strncpy(info->cwd_path, \"[unreadable]\", sizeof(info->cwd_path) - 1);","        info->cwd_path[sizeof(info->cwd_path) - 1] = '\\0';","    }","","    /* 6. Count open file descriptors in /proc/<pid>/fd/ */","    snprintf(path, sizeof(path), \"/proc/%d/fd\", (int)pid);","    DIR *dir_fd = opendir(path);","    info->open_fd_count = 0;","    if (dir_fd) {","        struct dirent *entry;","        while ((entry = readdir(dir_fd)) != NULL) {","            if (strcmp(entry->d_name, \".\") != 0 && strcmp(entry->d_name, \"..\") != 0) {","                info->open_fd_count++;","            }","        }","        closedir(dir_fd);","    }","","    return true;","}","","static void format_seconds(double total_sec, char *buf, size_t buf_size) {","    long sec = (long)total_sec;","    long hours = sec / 3600;","    long mins = (sec % 3600) / 60;","    long secs = sec % 60;","    if (hours > 0) {","        snprintf(buf, buf_size, \"%ldh %02ldm %02lds\", hours, mins, secs);","    } else if (mins > 0) {","        snprintf(buf, buf_size, \"%ldm %02lds\", mins, secs);","    } else {","        snprintf(buf, buf_size, \"%.2fs\", total_sec);","    }","}","","int builtin_inspect(int argc, char **argv) {","    if (argc < 2) {","        forge_error(\"inspect: missing PID argument. Usage: inspect <pid>\");","        return 1;","    }","","    pid_t target_pid = (pid_t)atoi(argv[1]);","    if (target_pid <= 0) {","        forge_error(\"inspect: invalid PID '%s'\", argv[1]);","        return 1;","    }","","    ProcInfo info;","    if (!proc_get_info(target_pid, &info)) {","        forge_error(\"inspect: process %d not found or terminated\", (int)target_pid);","        return 1;","    }","","    char runtime_str[64];","    char cpu_str[64];","    format_seconds(info.process_runtime_sec, runtime_str, sizeof(runtime_str));","    format_seconds(info.total_cpu_sec, cpu_str, sizeof(cpu_str));","","    printf(\"\\n%sProcess Inspection Report for PID %d%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD COLOR_CYAN, (int)target_pid, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"------------------------------------------------------------\\n\");","    printf(\"%s[General Identity]%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  Command Name:       %-20s  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [comm])\\n\", info.comm, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Process ID (PID):   %-20d  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [pid])\\n\", (int)info.pid, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Parent PID (PPID):  %-20d  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [ppid])\\n\", (int)info.ppid, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Process Group PGID: %-20d  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [pgrp])\\n\", (int)info.pgrp, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Session ID:         %-20d  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [session])\\n\", (int)info.session, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Process State:      %-20s  (Source: /proc/%d/status [State])\\n\", info.state_desc, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Thread Count:       %-20ld  (Source: /proc/%d/status [Threads])\\n\", info.num_threads, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Open File Descr.:   %-20d  (Source: /proc/%d/fd/ directory count)\\n\", info.open_fd_count, (int)target_pid);","","    printf(\"\\n%s[Memory Footprint]%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  Virtual Size (VmSize): %8lu kB (%6.2f MB) (Source: /proc/%d/status [VmSize])\\n\",","           info.vm_size_kb, (double)info.vm_size_kb / 1024.0, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Resident Set (VmRSS):  %8lu kB (%6.2f MB) (Source: /proc/%d/status [VmRSS])\\n\",","           info.vm_rss_kb, (double)info.vm_rss_kb / 1024.0, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Data Segment (VmData): %8lu kB            (Source: /proc/%d/status [VmData])\\n\",","           info.vm_data_kb, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Stack Segment (VmStk): %8lu kB            (Source: /proc/%d/status [VmStk])\\n\",","           info.vm_stk_kb, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Text Segment (VmExe):  %8lu kB            (Source: /proc/%d/status [VmExe])\\n\",","           info.vm_exe_kb, (int)target_pid);","","    printf(\"\\n%s[CPU Usage & Timing]%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  User CPU Time:         %12lu ticks  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [utime])\\n\",","           info.utime_ticks, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  System CPU Time:       %12lu ticks  (Source: /proc/%d/stat [stime])\\n\",","           info.stime_ticks, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Total CPU Executed:    %12s        (Source: (utime+stime) / CLK_TCK)\\n\",","           cpu_str);","    printf(\"  Process Wall Runtime:  %12s        (Source: /proc/uptime - starttime)\\n\",","           runtime_str);","","    printf(\"\\n%s[Paths & Invocation]%s\\n\", COLOR_BOLD, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"  Executable Binary:  %s  (Source: readlink /proc/%d/exe)\\n\", info.exe_path, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Working Directory:  %s  (Source: readlink /proc/%d/cwd)\\n\", info.cwd_path, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"  Command Line:       %s  (Source: /proc/%d/cmdline)\\n\", info.cmdline, (int)target_pid);","    printf(\"------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n\");","","    proc_free_info(&info);","    return 0;","}","","int builtin_monitor(int argc, char **argv) {","    if (argc < 2) {","        forge_error(\"monitor: missing PID argument. Usage: monitor <pid> [interval_sec]\");","        return 1;","    }","","    pid_t target_pid = (pid_t)atoi(argv[1]);","    if (target_pid <= 0) {","        forge_error(\"monitor: invalid PID '%s'\", argv[1]);","        return 1;","    }","","    int interval_sec = 1;","    if (argc > 2) {","        interval_sec = atoi(argv[2]);","        if (interval_sec <= 0) interval_sec = 1;","    }","","    ProcInfo prev_info;","    if (!proc_get_info(target_pid, &prev_info)) {","        forge_error(\"monitor: process %d not found or terminated\", (int)target_pid);","        return 1;","    }","","    long clk_tck = sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK);","    if (clk_tck <= 0) clk_tck = 100;","","    printf(\"\\n%sMonitoring PID %d (%s) - sampling every %d second(s)%s\\n\",","           COLOR_BOLD COLOR_GREEN, (int)target_pid, prev_info.comm, interval_sec, COLOR_RESET);","    printf(\"Press Ctrl+C to stop monitoring and return to prompt.\\n\\n\");","    printf(\"%-8s %-5s %-4s %-7s %-12s %-12s %-7s %-10s\\n\",","           \"TIME\", \"PID\", \"STAT\", \"CPU %\", \"VmRSS (MB)\", \"VmSize (MB)\", \"THREADS\", \"RUNTIME\");","    printf(\"-----------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\");","","    /* Install temporary SIGINT handler so user can interrupt the monitor loop */","    g_monitor_interrupted = 0;","    struct sigaction sa_old, sa_new;","    memset(&sa_new, 0, sizeof(sa_new));","    sa_new.sa_handler = monitor_sigint_handler;","    sigaction(SIGINT, &sa_new, &sa_old);","","    struct timespec prev_time;","    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &prev_time);","","    while (!g_monitor_interrupted) {","        /* Sleep for interval using nanosleep (which is interrupted cleanly by SIGINT) */","        struct timespec req = {.tv_sec = interval_sec, .tv_nsec = 0};","        while (nanosleep(&req, &req) == -1 && errno == EINTR) {","            if (g_monitor_interrupted) break;","        }","","        if (g_monitor_interrupted) break;","","        struct timespec curr_time;","        clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &curr_time);","","        ProcInfo curr_info;","        if (!proc_get_info(target_pid, &curr_info)) {","            printf(\"\\n%sforge: monitor: process %d has terminated.%s\\n\",","                   COLOR_YELLOW, (int)target_pid, COLOR_RESET);","            proc_free_info(&prev_info);","            sigaction(SIGINT, &sa_old, NULL);","            return 0;","        }","","        double elapsed_sec = (curr_time.tv_sec - prev_time.tv_sec) +","                             (curr_time.tv_nsec - prev_time.tv_nsec) / 1e9;","        if (elapsed_sec <= 0.0) elapsed_sec = (double)interval_sec;","","        unsigned long delta_ticks = (curr_info.utime_ticks + curr_info.stime_ticks) -","                                    (prev_info.utime_ticks + prev_info.stime_ticks);","        double cpu_pct = ((double)delta_ticks / (double)clk_tck) / elapsed_sec * 100.0;","        if (cpu_pct < 0.0) cpu_pct = 0.0;","","        time_t raw_time = time(NULL);","        struct tm *tm_info = localtime(&raw_time);","        char time_str[16];","        strftime(time_str, sizeof(time_str), \"%H:%M:%S\", tm_info);","","        char runtime_str[32];","        format_seconds(curr_info.process_runtime_sec, runtime_str, sizeof(runtime_str));","","        printf(\"%-8s %-5d %-4c %6.1f%% %10.2fMB %10.2fMB %-7ld %-10s\\n\",","               time_str,","               (int)curr_info.pid,","               curr_info.state,","               cpu_pct,","               (double)curr_info.vm_rss_kb / 1024.0,","               (double)curr_info.vm_size_kb / 1024.0,","               curr_info.num_threads,","               runtime_str);","        fflush(stdout);","","        proc_free_info(&prev_info);","        prev_info = curr_info;","        prev_time = curr_time;","    }","","    proc_free_info(&prev_info);","    sigaction(SIGINT, &sa_old, NULL);","    printf(\"\\n%sMonitoring stopped.%s\\n\", COLOR_DIM, COLOR_RESET);","    return 0;","}","","int builtin_ps(int argc, char **argv) {","    (void)argc;","    (void)argv;","","    DIR *proc_dir = opendir(\"/proc\");","    if (!proc_dir) {","        forge_perror(\"opendir /proc\");","        return 1;","    }","","    printf(\"%-6s %-6s %-4s %-7s %-10s %-10s %s\\n\",","           \"PID\", \"PPID\", \"STAT\", \"THREADS\", \"VmRSS\", \"TIME\", \"COMMAND\");","    printf(\"------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\");","","    struct dirent *entry;","    while ((entry = readdir(proc_dir)) != NULL) {","        /* Check if directory name is numeric (PID) */","        char *endptr;","        long pid = strtol(entry->d_name, &endptr, 10);","        if (*endptr != '\\0' || pid <= 0) {","            continue;","        }","","        ProcInfo info;","        if (proc_get_info((pid_t)pid, &info)) {","            char time_str[32];","            format_seconds(info.total_cpu_sec, time_str, sizeof(time_str));","","            char rss_str[32];","            if (info.vm_rss_kb >= 1024) {","                snprintf(rss_str, sizeof(rss_str), \"%.1fMB\", (double)info.vm_rss_kb / 1024.0);","            } else {","                snprintf(rss_str, sizeof(rss_str), \"%luKB\", info.vm_rss_kb);","            }","","            printf(\"%-6d %-6d %-4c %-7ld %-10s %-10s %s\\n\",","                   (int)info.pid,","                   (int)info.ppid,","                   info.state,","                   info.num_threads,","                   rss_str,","                   time_str,","                   info.comm);","            proc_free_info(&info);","        }","    }","","    closedir(proc_dir);","    return 0;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":5,"html":"<p><code>dirent.h</code> is for listing directories (<code>/proc</code> and <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd</code>), <code>time.h</code> for clocks and timestamps, and <code>signal.h</code> for the temporary Ctrl+C handler that <code>monitor</code> installs.</p>"},{"start":6,"end":12,"html":"<p><code>monitor</code> loops until Ctrl+C. The shell normally <em>ignores</em> <code>SIGINT</code>, so <code>monitor</code> swaps in a handler that only sets this flag, and the loop checks it. It's the same async-signal-safe flag pattern as in <code>signals.c</code>, and here the flag is actually read.</p><p><code>monitor</code> is a builtin, so it runs <em>inside the shell process</em>, which is the foreground group at that moment. That's why Ctrl+C reaches the shell here at all.</p>"},{"start":13,"end":26,"html":"<p>Converts the single-letter state from <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stat</code> into words. The interesting ones for interviews:</p><p><code>R</code> running or ready to run. <code>S</code> sleeping until an event (most processes, most of the time). <code>D</code> uninterruptible sleep, usually waiting on disk I/O, and even <code>SIGKILL</code> can't interrupt it. <code>Z</code> zombie: finished, but the parent hasn't collected its exit status. <code>T</code> stopped by Ctrl+Z or <code>SIGSTOP</code>.</p>"},{"start":27,"end":37,"html":"<p><code>/proc/uptime</code> holds two numbers: seconds since boot, then idle time summed over all CPUs. Only the first is read. It's needed because a process's start time in <code>/proc</code> is recorded as \"ticks after boot\", not as a date.</p>"},{"start":38,"end":45,"html":"<p><code>cmdline</code> is the only heap-allocated field in <code>ProcInfo</code> (its length isn't known in advance). Everything else is fixed-size and lives inside the struct.</p>"},{"start":46,"end":52,"html":"<p>The core reader. It fills a <code>ProcInfo</code> from six sources under <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/</code>. <code>memset</code> zeroes the struct first, so any field that can't be read stays 0 instead of garbage.</p><p><code>/proc</code> isn't on disk. Every read is answered by the kernel on the spot from its live process tables, so the values are current to that moment.</p>"},{"start":53,"end":66,"html":"<p><code>stat</code> is one line of about 52 space-separated fields. If it can't be opened, the process doesn't exist (or has exited), and that's how <code>inspect</code> reports \"not found\".</p>"},{"start":67,"end":73,"html":"<p><strong>The classic trap.</strong> Field 2 is the command name in parentheses, and the name may itself contain spaces or <code>)</code>: a program can call itself <code>a) b</code>. Splitting on spaces would shift every later field. The safe approach, used here and by <code>procps</code>, is to take everything between the <em>first</em> <code>(</code> and the <em>last</em> <code>)</code>, because the kernel never puts a <code>)</code> after the name.</p>"},{"start":74,"end":78,"html":"<p>Copies the name into the fixed 256-byte <code>comm</code> field, truncating if needed, and always adds the terminating <code>'\\0'</code> (<code>strncpy</code> doesn't add one when it truncates).</p>"},{"start":79,"end":92,"html":"<p><code>close_paren + 2</code> skips <code>) </code> and lands on field 3. In the <code>sscanf</code> format, <code>%*d</code> / <code>%*u</code> means \"read and discard\". Field by field:</p><p>3 state, 4 ppid, 5 pgrp, 6 session, (7 tty, 8 tpgid, 9 flags, 10–13 page-fault counters skipped), 14 <strong>utime</strong>, 15 <strong>stime</strong>, (16–19 skipped), 20 <strong>num_threads</strong>, (21 skipped), 22 <strong>starttime</strong>.</p><p><code>sscanf</code> returns how many values it stored. Fewer than 8 means the format didn't match, so the read fails cleanly.</p>"},{"start":93,"end":102,"html":"<p>Copies the parsed values into the struct.</p>"},{"start":103,"end":110,"html":"<p>CPU times in <code>/proc</code> are in <strong>clock ticks</strong>, not seconds. <code>sysconf(_SC_CLK_TCK)</code> gives ticks per second (100 on almost every Linux system), with 100 as a fallback.</p><p>Total CPU = (utime + stime) / ticks per second. Wall-clock runtime = uptime − start time, where start time is in ticks since boot. A busy process has CPU time close to its runtime, and a sleeping one has almost none. In the demo, <code>spin</code> uses about one second of CPU per second, while <code>leak</code> uses a fraction of a second in total.</p>"},{"start":111,"end":126,"html":"<p><code>status</code> is the human-readable version: one <code>Key:\\tvalue</code> per line. It's read line by line, and only the memory lines and <code>Threads</code> are kept:</p><p><strong>VmSize</strong> is all the virtual memory the process has <em>reserved</em>. <strong>VmRSS</strong> (resident set size) is how much of it is actually in RAM right now. <strong>VmData</strong> is heap and data, <strong>VmStk</strong> the main thread's stack, <strong>VmExe</strong> the program's code. In the demo, <code>malloc(300 MB)</code> alone raises VmSize. Only <em>writing</em> to the pages raises VmRSS, because Linux hands out physical memory lazily, page by page.</p>"},{"start":127,"end":136,"html":"<p><code>Threads</code> is also in <code>stat</code> (field 20), and this overwrites it with the same number from <code>status</code>. Using either one would have been enough.</p>"},{"start":137,"end":158,"html":"<p><code>cmdline</code> is the program's arguments separated by <code>'\\0'</code> bytes, exactly as they sit in its memory. Replacing every NUL except the last with a space makes it printable. Kernel threads (and zombies) have an empty <code>cmdline</code>, so the <code>comm</code> name is used instead. It's opened <code>\"rb\"</code> because the content is binary.</p>"},{"start":159,"end":168,"html":"<p><code>exe</code> is a symbolic link to the program file being run. <code>readlink</code> doesn't add a terminating <code>'\\0'</code>, so it's added by hand using the returned length. Reading another user's <code>exe</code> needs extra permissions, hence the fallback text.</p>"},{"start":169,"end":178,"html":"<p>Same idea for the process's current directory.</p>"},{"start":179,"end":195,"html":"<p><code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd/</code> has one entry per open descriptor (<code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, ...), so counting entries, minus <code>.</code> and <code>..</code>, counts open files. That's how you'd spot a descriptor leak. The test suite uses the same trick on <code>/proc/self/fd</code>.</p>"},{"start":196,"end":209,"html":"<p>Formats a duration as <code>1h 02m 03s</code>, <code>2m 05s</code> or <code>4.27s</code>. The <code>%02ld</code> pads minutes and seconds to two digits.</p>"},{"start":210,"end":227,"html":"<p><code>inspect &lt;pid&gt;</code> validates the argument (<code>atoi</code> returns 0 for junk, so 0 and negatives are rejected), reads everything with <code>proc_get_info</code>, and prints a report.</p>"},{"start":228,"end":244,"html":"<p>The report prints the <code>/proc</code> source next to every value, so whoever reads it can check the number themselves with <code>cat /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code>. One small inaccuracy: the \"Process State\" line says it comes from <code>status [State]</code>, but the value is actually parsed from <code>stat</code> field 3. Both files report the same state.</p>"},{"start":245,"end":256,"html":"<p>Memory in kB as reported by the kernel, plus MB for easier reading (÷ 1024).</p>"},{"start":257,"end":266,"html":"<p>Raw ticks plus the computed totals. <em>User</em> time is spent running the program's own code, <em>system</em> time is spent in the kernel on its behalf. In the demo, nearly all of <code>leak</code>'s few ticks are system time: the kernel handing it 300 MB of fresh pages, one page fault at a time.</p>"},{"start":267,"end":276,"html":"<p>Program path, working directory and full command line. Freeing <code>cmdline</code> at the end keeps <code>inspect</code> from leaking memory each time it runs.</p>"},{"start":277,"end":294,"html":"<p><code>monitor &lt;pid&gt; [seconds]</code> samples a process repeatedly and prints a row each interval. The interval defaults to 1 second, and nonsense values fall back to 1.</p>"},{"start":295,"end":310,"html":"<p>An initial sample is taken before the loop. CPU % needs <strong>two</strong> samples, because <code>/proc</code> only gives total CPU time since the process started. Usage <em>right now</em> is the difference between two samples divided by the time between them.</p>"},{"start":311,"end":317,"html":"<p><code>sigaction(SIGINT, &amp;sa_new, &amp;sa_old)</code> installs the flag-setting handler and saves the old setting (<code>SIG_IGN</code>) so it can be put back on every exit path. Because <code>sa_flags</code> is 0 (no <code>SA_RESTART</code>), Ctrl+C makes <code>nanosleep</code> return early with <code>EINTR</code>, so the loop stops at once instead of finishing the current interval.</p>"},{"start":318,"end":320,"html":"<p><code>CLOCK_MONOTONIC</code> is used for measuring intervals. It only ever moves forward, unlike the wall clock, which can jump when the date is set or NTP adjusts it.</p>"},{"start":321,"end":329,"html":"<p><code>nanosleep</code> may be cut short by a signal. It then writes the <em>remaining</em> time into <code>req</code>, and the inner loop sleeps again for the rest, unless the interruption was our Ctrl+C.</p>"},{"start":330,"end":341,"html":"<p>Take a new sample. If the process has gone away, <code>monitor</code> says so, restores the old <code>SIGINT</code> setting and returns.</p>"},{"start":342,"end":350,"html":"<p><strong>CPU % = (Δticks ÷ ticks per second) ÷ elapsed seconds × 100.</strong> If the process used 100 ticks (1 s of CPU) during 1 s of real time, that's 100%, which is what <code>spin</code> shows in the demo. A process with several threads can go above 100%, one core's worth per thread.</p><p><code>delta_ticks</code> is unsigned, so the <code>cpu_pct &lt; 0</code> check can never trigger. It's purely defensive.</p>"},{"start":351,"end":358,"html":"<p>A wall-clock timestamp for each row, formatted as <code>HH:MM:SS</code>.</p>"},{"start":359,"end":369,"html":"<p>One row per sample: time, PID, state, CPU %, RSS and virtual size in MB, threads, runtime. <code>fflush</code> makes each row appear as soon as it's printed.</p>"},{"start":370,"end":376,"html":"<p>The new sample becomes the old one for the next round. The old sample's <code>cmdline</code> is freed first, then <code>prev_info = curr_info</code> copies the struct, <em>including</em> the <code>cmdline</code> pointer. Ownership of that string moves to <code>prev_info</code>, so exactly one live copy of each string exists at any time.</p><p>After the loop ends (Ctrl+C), the last sample is freed and the shell's original <code>SIGINT</code> setting (ignore) is put back.</p>"},{"start":377,"end":380,"html":"<p>Back to the prompt. A design point worth mentioning: every exit path from <code>monitor</code> restores the old <code>SIGINT</code> setting. If one path forgot, the shell would be left with the temporary handler, and Ctrl+C at the prompt would set a flag nobody reads.</p>"},{"start":381,"end":394,"html":"<p>A minimal <code>ps</code>. Every running process has a directory in <code>/proc</code> named after its PID, so listing <code>/proc</code> and keeping the numeric names lists every process.</p>"},{"start":395,"end":403,"html":"<p><code>strtol</code> with <code>endptr</code> checks that the <em>whole</em> name is a number (<code>*endptr == '\\0'</code>). That skips entries like <code>self</code>, <code>cpuinfo</code> and <code>sys</code>.</p>"},{"start":404,"end":427,"html":"<p>Each PID is read with the same <code>proc_get_info</code>. A process can exit between listing the directory and reading its files. That's a normal race, and <code>proc_get_info</code> returning false just skips it. RSS is shown in KB or MB depending on size.</p>"},{"start":428,"end":430,"html":"<p>Closes the directory handle. Without this, every <code>ps</code> would leak one descriptor.</p>"}]},{"path":"src/common.c","title":"Message helpers shared by every file: plain log, colored error, errno error, and debug output.","group":"Shell core","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"common.h\"","","void forge_log(const char *fmt, ...) {","    va_list args;","    va_start(args, fmt);","    fprintf(stderr, \"forge: \");","    vfprintf(stderr, fmt, args);","    fprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");","    va_end(args);","}","","void forge_error(const char *fmt, ...) {","    va_list args;","    va_start(args, fmt);","    if (isatty(STDERR_FILENO)) {","        fprintf(stderr, \"%sforge: error: %s\", COLOR_RED, COLOR_RESET);","    } else {","        fprintf(stderr, \"forge: error: \");","    }","    vfprintf(stderr, fmt, args);","    fprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");","    va_end(args);","}","","void forge_perror(const char *msg) {","    if (isatty(STDERR_FILENO)) {","        fprintf(stderr, \"%sforge: %s: %s%s\\n\", COLOR_RED, msg, strerror(errno), COLOR_RESET);","    } else {","        fprintf(stderr, \"forge: %s: %s\\n\", msg, strerror(errno));","    }","}","","void forge_debug(const char *fmt, ...) {","#ifdef DEBUG","    va_list args;","    va_start(args, fmt);","    if (isatty(STDERR_FILENO)) {","        fprintf(stderr, \"%s[debug] \", COLOR_DIM);","        vfprintf(stderr, fmt, args);","        fprintf(stderr, \"%s\\n\", COLOR_RESET);","    } else {","        fprintf(stderr, \"[debug] \");","        vfprintf(stderr, fmt, args);","        fprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");","    }","    va_end(args);","#else","    (void)fmt;","#endif","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":11,"html":"<p><code>forge_log</code> is a <code>printf</code>-style function that writes <code>forge: &lt;message&gt;</code> to stderr. The <code>...</code> and <code>va_list</code> / <code>va_start</code> / <code>vfprintf</code> / <code>va_end</code> are how C passes a variable number of arguments on to another printf-style function. Nothing calls <code>forge_log</code> at the moment.</p>"},{"start":12,"end":24,"html":"<p>Used for errors the shell itself detects (syntax errors, bad arguments). Messages always go to <strong>stderr</strong>, never stdout, so <code>cmd &gt; out.txt</code> doesn't capture the shell's complaints, and tests can check errors and output separately. The label is red only when stderr is a terminal, so log files never get escape codes in them.</p>"},{"start":25,"end":32,"html":"<p>For failed system calls. <code>strerror(errno)</code> turns the error number the kernel set into text like \"No such file or directory\". Like the C library's own <code>perror()</code>, it must be called <em>right after</em> the failing call, before anything else can overwrite <code>errno</code>.</p>"},{"start":33,"end":50,"html":"<p>Debug messages compiled in only for <code>make debug</code> and <code>make asan</code>, which define <code>DEBUG</code>. In a normal build the body is removed by the preprocessor, and <code>(void)fmt</code> silences the unused-parameter warning. No call sites use it today, but it's ready for tracing.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/common.h","title":"Shared includes, constants, colors and the out-of-memory-safe allocation wrappers.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_COMMON_H","#define FORGESHELL_COMMON_H","","#ifndef _POSIX_C_SOURCE","#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L","#endif","","#ifndef _GNU_SOURCE","#define _GNU_SOURCE","#endif","","#include <stdio.h>","#include <stdlib.h>","#include <string.h>","#include <unistd.h>","#include <stdbool.h>","#include <errno.h>","#include <sys/types.h>","#include <limits.h>","","#define FORGE_VERSION \"0.1.0\"","#define FORGE_DEFAULT_PROMPT \"forge> \"","#define FORGE_MAX_INPUT 8192","","/* ANSI Terminal Colors (used when stdout/stderr isatty) */","#define COLOR_RESET   \"\\033[0m\"","#define COLOR_BOLD    \"\\033[1m\"","#define COLOR_DIM     \"\\033[2m\"","#define COLOR_RED     \"\\033[31m\"","#define COLOR_GREEN   \"\\033[32m\"","#define COLOR_YELLOW  \"\\033[33m\"","#define COLOR_BLUE    \"\\033[34m\"","#define COLOR_CYAN    \"\\033[36m\"","","/* Diagnostic and error helpers */","void forge_log(const char *fmt, ...);","void forge_error(const char *fmt, ...);","void forge_perror(const char *msg);","void forge_debug(const char *fmt, ...);","","","#include <stdarg.h>","","/* Safe memory management helpers */","static inline void *xmalloc(size_t size) {","    void *ptr = malloc(size);","    if (!ptr && size > 0) {","        forge_error(\"out of memory (malloc failed for %zu bytes)\", size);","        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);","    }","    return ptr;","}","","static inline void *xrealloc(void *ptr, size_t size) {","    void *new_ptr = realloc(ptr, size);","    if (!new_ptr && size > 0) {","        forge_error(\"out of memory (realloc failed for %zu bytes)\", size);","        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);","    }","    return new_ptr;","}","","static inline char *xstrdup(const char *s) {","    if (!s) return NULL;","    char *dup = strdup(s);","    if (!dup) {","        forge_error(\"out of memory (strdup failed)\");","        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);","    }","    return dup;","}","","static inline void safe_free(void **ptr) {","    if (ptr && *ptr) {","        free(*ptr);","        *ptr = NULL;","    }","}","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_COMMON_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":3,"html":"<p>An <em>include guard</em>: the first time this header is included, the macro gets defined. Any later include in the same file is skipped, so nothing is declared twice.</p>"},{"start":4,"end":11,"html":"<p><strong>Feature-test macros.</strong> With <code>-std=c11</code>, glibc hides anything that isn't plain ISO C, including <code>getline</code>, <code>strdup</code>, <code>sigaction</code> and <code>kill</code>. Defining <code>_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L</code> (POSIX 2008) and <code>_GNU_SOURCE</code> turns those back on. The Makefile already passes both with <code>-D</code>, and the <code>#ifndef</code> checks avoid redefinition warnings. They must come before any system header is included.</p>"},{"start":12,"end":20,"html":"<p>The headers almost every file needs. <code>stdbool.h</code> gives <code>bool</code>, <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>. <code>limits.h</code> gives <code>PATH_MAX</code> (4096 on Linux), used to size path buffers.</p>"},{"start":21,"end":24,"html":"<p>Version string (shown by <code>help</code>), the default prompt, and a maximum input size. <code>FORGE_MAX_INPUT</code> and <code>FORGE_DEFAULT_PROMPT</code> aren't used: <code>getline</code> handles any line length, and the prompt is printed directly in <code>main.c</code>.</p>"},{"start":25,"end":34,"html":"<p>ANSI escape sequences: <code>ESC [ &lt;code&gt; m</code>. <code>\\033</code> is ESC in octal. The terminal reads them as style changes instead of printing them. Code 0 resets, 1 is bold, 2 dim, and 31–36 set the text color. They're only printed when the output is a terminal (<code>isatty</code>).</p>"},{"start":35,"end":41,"html":"<p>Declarations for the helpers in <code>src/common.c</code>.</p>"},{"start":42,"end":44,"html":"<p>Needed for <code>va_list</code> in the helpers' implementations. It sits mid-file only because of where it was added. The usual style is to put all includes at the top.</p>"},{"start":45,"end":53,"html":"<p><strong>The x-functions.</strong> <code>malloc</code> can return <code>NULL</code>, and checking every call site is noisy and easy to forget. <code>xmalloc</code> checks once and exits with a message if memory runs out. For an interactive shell that's a reasonable policy: running out of memory here is extremely unlikely, and quitting cleanly beats crashing on a <code>NULL</code> pointer later. <code>static inline</code> in a header gives each source file its own copy without linker conflicts.</p><p>The <code>size &gt; 0</code> check exists because <code>malloc(0)</code> may legitimately return <code>NULL</code>.</p>"},{"start":54,"end":62,"html":"<p>Same idea for <code>realloc</code>. If <code>realloc</code> fails, the old block is still valid, but here the program exits anyway, so it doesn't matter.</p>"},{"start":63,"end":72,"html":"<p><code>strdup</code> with a failure check. Passing <code>NULL</code> returns <code>NULL</code> instead of crashing, which the token list relies on for <code>TOKEN_EOF</code>.</p>"},{"start":73,"end":79,"html":"<p>Frees a pointer and sets it to <code>NULL</code>, so a second free becomes a harmless <code>free(NULL)</code>. Nothing calls it today. The code frees directly, and resets pointers by hand where it matters.</p>"},{"start":80,"end":80,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/parser.h","title":"The data structures a command line becomes: Command and JobPipeline.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_PARSER_H","#define FORGESHELL_PARSER_H","","#include \"common.h\"","","/* Single command in a pipeline */","typedef struct Command {","    char **argv;           /* NULL-terminated argument vector */","    int argc;","    char *input_file;      /* < file */","    char *output_file;     /* > or >> file */","    bool append_output;    /* true if >>, false if > */","    char *error_file;      /* 2> file */","    struct Command *next;  /* next command in pipeline (connected via |) */","} Command;","","/* Complete parsed pipeline representing a job */","typedef struct JobPipeline {","    Command *first_cmd;    /* linked list of commands */","    bool is_background;    /* trailing & */","    char *raw_line;        /* original line text for job reporting */","} JobPipeline;","","/* Parse an input line into a JobPipeline. Returns NULL on syntax error or empty input. */","JobPipeline *parse_pipeline(const char *input_line);","","/* Free an allocated JobPipeline and all its commands and arguments */","void free_pipeline(JobPipeline *pipeline);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_PARSER_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":5,"html":"<p>Include guard, plus <code>common.h</code> for <code>bool</code> and the basic types.</p>"},{"start":6,"end":16,"html":"<p>One stage of a pipeline, such as <code>grep -v dog &gt; out.txt</code>:</p><p><code>argv</code> / <code>argc</code>: the program name and its arguments, ending with <code>NULL</code> so it can go straight to <code>execvp</code>. Then one optional file name per redirect: <code>input_file</code> for <code>&lt;</code>, <code>output_file</code> for <code>&gt;</code> or <code>&gt;&gt;</code> (with <code>append_output</code> telling them apart), and <code>error_file</code> for <code>2&gt;</code>. <code>next</code> links to the following stage, so <code>a | b | c</code> is a linked list of three <code>Command</code>s.</p>"},{"start":17,"end":23,"html":"<p>The whole line: the first command, whether it ended in <code>&amp;</code>, and the original text (shown by <code>jobs</code>). One <code>JobPipeline</code> becomes one job, and one process group, when it runs.</p>"},{"start":24,"end":29,"html":"<p>The parser's public interface is just these two functions. Every other parser function is <code>static</code> (file-private), so the tokenizer can change freely without touching any other file.</p>"},{"start":30,"end":30,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/executor.h","title":"Global shell state and the executor's public functions.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_EXECUTOR_H","#define FORGESHELL_EXECUTOR_H","","#include \"common.h\"","#include \"parser.h\"","#include \"builtins.h\"","#include \"jobs.h\"","#include \"signals.h\"","#include <sys/wait.h>","#include <fcntl.h>","#include <termios.h>","","/* Shell execution state */","typedef struct ShellState {","    int last_exit_status;","    bool is_interactive;","    pid_t shell_pgid;","    int shell_terminal;","    struct termios shell_tmodes;","} ShellState;","","extern ShellState g_shell;","","/* Initialize shell environment, job control, and terminal signals */","void shell_init(void);","","/* Apply I/O redirections for a single command. Returns 0 on success, -1 on failure. */","int apply_redirections(const Command *cmd);","","/* Execute a single external command, builtin, or pipeline */","int execute_pipeline(JobPipeline *pipeline);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_EXECUTOR_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":12,"html":"<p>The executor header pulls in the system headers for processes and terminals: <code>sys/wait.h</code> (<code>waitpid</code> and the <code>W*</code> macros), <code>fcntl.h</code> (<code>open</code> and the <code>O_*</code> flags), <code>termios.h</code> (<code>struct termios</code>, <code>tcsetattr</code>).</p>"},{"start":13,"end":21,"html":"<p>Everything the shell needs to remember between commands. <code>shell_terminal</code> is normally descriptor 0, and <code>shell_tmodes</code> is the snapshot of terminal settings taken at startup and restored after every foreground job.</p>"},{"start":22,"end":23,"html":"<p><code>extern</code> <em>declares</em> the global without creating it. It's defined once, in <code>executor.c</code>. Any file that includes this header (<code>jobs.c</code>, <code>builtins.c</code>) can use it.</p>"},{"start":24,"end":32,"html":"<p>The three entry points: one-time setup, applying redirects to the current process, and running a parsed pipeline.</p>"},{"start":33,"end":33,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/builtins.h","title":"The builtin command interface.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_BUILTINS_H","#define FORGESHELL_BUILTINS_H","","#include \"common.h\"","","/* Returns true if the command name is a ForgeShell builtin */","bool is_builtin(const char *name);","","/* Execute a builtin command in-process. Returns exit status (0 for success). */","int execute_builtin(int argc, char **argv);","","/* Builtin command handlers */","int builtin_cd(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_pwd(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_exit(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_export(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_unset(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_help(int argc, char **argv);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_BUILTINS_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":5,"html":"<p>Include guard and shared includes.</p>"},{"start":6,"end":19,"html":"<p><code>is_builtin</code> lets the executor decide whether to fork, and <code>execute_builtin</code> dispatches by name. Every builtin has the same signature as <code>main</code> (<code>argc</code>, <code>argv</code>) and returns an exit status, so it behaves like a small program that happens to run inside the shell. The job-control and <code>/proc</code> builtins are declared in <code>jobs.h</code> and <code>proc.h</code>, next to the code they belong to.</p>"},{"start":20,"end":20,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/jobs.h","title":"Job and Process records and the job-control API.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_JOBS_H","#define FORGESHELL_JOBS_H","","#include \"common.h\"","#include <termios.h>","","typedef enum {","    JOB_STATE_RUNNING,","    JOB_STATE_STOPPED,","    JOB_STATE_DONE","} JobState;","","typedef struct Process {","    pid_t pid;","    JobState state;","    int status;","    bool completed;","    bool stopped;","    struct Process *next;","} Process;","","typedef struct Job {","    int id;","    pid_t pgid;","    JobState state;","    char *command_str;","    Process *first_process;","    struct termios tmodes;","    bool notified;","    struct Job *next;","} Job;","","/* Job manager functions */","void jobs_init(void);","Job *job_create(pid_t pgid, const char *cmd_str);","void job_add_process(Job *job, pid_t pid);","Job *job_find_by_id(int id);","Job *job_find_by_pgid(pid_t pgid);","Job *job_get_most_recent(void);","void job_remove(Job *job);","void job_free(Job *job);","","/* Job control actions */","int wait_for_job(Job *job);","int put_job_in_foreground(Job *job, bool cont);","int put_job_in_background(Job *job, bool cont);","void check_and_notify_jobs(void);","","/* Check if all processes in job completed */","bool job_is_completed(const Job *job);","bool job_is_stopped(const Job *job);","","/* Builtin job commands */","int builtin_jobs(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_fg(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_bg(int argc, char **argv);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_JOBS_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p><code>termios.h</code> is included because each <code>Job</code> has room for its own terminal settings.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":12,"html":"<p>The three states a job or process can be in: running, stopped (Ctrl+Z, <code>SIGTTIN</code>), or done.</p>"},{"start":13,"end":21,"html":"<p>One OS process, one pipeline stage. <code>status</code> holds the raw <code>waitpid</code> status word. <code>completed</code> and <code>stopped</code> are the flags the job-level checks combine. The <code>struct Process</code> name before the typedef lets <code>next</code> refer to the type while it's still being defined.</p>"},{"start":22,"end":32,"html":"<p>One job, meaning one process group. <code>pgid</code> is the group every process in the job belongs to, and it's what <code>tcsetpgrp</code>, <code>kill(-pgid)</code> and <code>waitpid(-pgid)</code> target. <code>command_str</code> is for display. <code>notified</code> makes sure a stop is reported only once.</p><p><code>tmodes</code> is never used: a full shell would save a stopped job's terminal settings here and restore them on <code>fg</code>, so <code>vim</code> comes back in the right mode. ForgeShell restores only the shell's own settings.</p>"},{"start":33,"end":42,"html":"<p>Table management: create, add a process, find, remove, free.</p>"},{"start":43,"end":48,"html":"<p>The job-control core: wait for a job, foreground or background it (<code>cont</code> = also send <code>SIGCONT</code>), and the before-prompt reaper.</p>"},{"start":49,"end":52,"html":"<p>Job-level state worked out from the per-process flags.</p>"},{"start":53,"end":57,"html":"<p><code>jobs</code>, <code>fg</code> and <code>bg</code> live in <code>jobs.c</code> because they need the private job list.</p>"},{"start":58,"end":58,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/signals.h","title":"Signal setup for the shell and its children.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_SIGNALS_H","#define FORGESHELL_SIGNALS_H","","#include \"common.h\"","#include <signal.h>","","extern volatile sig_atomic_t g_sigchld_flag;","","/* Set up signal disposition for the interactive shell process */","void signals_init_shell(void);","","/* Reset signal dispositions to default in child process prior to exec */","void signals_reset_child(void);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_SIGNALS_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p>Include guard, plus <code>signal.h</code> for <code>sigaction</code>, <code>sigset_t</code> and the signal numbers.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":8,"html":"<p>The flag the <code>SIGCHLD</code> handler sets, declared <code>extern</code> so other files <em>could</em> read it. None do (see <code>signals.c</code>).</p>"},{"start":9,"end":14,"html":"<p>Two calls, one for each side of <code>fork()</code>: <code>signals_init_shell</code> once at startup in the shell, and <code>signals_reset_child</code> in every child before <code>exec</code>.</p>"},{"start":15,"end":15,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"include/proc.h","title":"ProcInfo: one snapshot of a process, assembled from several /proc files.","group":"Headers","lang":"c","lines":["#ifndef FORGESHELL_PROC_H","#define FORGESHELL_PROC_H","","#include \"common.h\"","","typedef struct ProcInfo {","    pid_t pid;","    pid_t ppid;","    pid_t pgrp;","    pid_t session;","    char comm[256];","    char state;","    const char *state_desc;","    long num_threads;","    ","    /* Memory metrics from /proc/<pid>/status */","    unsigned long vm_size_kb;","    unsigned long vm_rss_kb;","    unsigned long vm_data_kb;","    unsigned long vm_stk_kb;","    unsigned long vm_exe_kb;","","    /* CPU metrics from /proc/<pid>/stat */","    unsigned long utime_ticks;","    unsigned long stime_ticks;","    unsigned long long starttime_ticks;","","    /* Runtime metrics */","    double total_cpu_sec;","    double process_runtime_sec;","","    /* Open file descriptors */","    int open_fd_count;","","    /* Paths */","    char *cmdline;","    char exe_path[PATH_MAX];","    char cwd_path[PATH_MAX];","} ProcInfo;","","/* Fetch process information from Linux /proc. Returns false if process does not exist. */","bool proc_get_info(pid_t pid, ProcInfo *info);","","/* Free allocated dynamic memory in ProcInfo */","void proc_free_info(ProcInfo *info);","","/* Get human-readable description for single-character process state */","const char *proc_state_description(char state);","","/* Builtin commands */","int builtin_inspect(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_monitor(int argc, char **argv);","int builtin_ps(int argc, char **argv);","","#endif /* FORGESHELL_PROC_H */"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":5,"html":"<p>Include guard and common types.</p>"},{"start":6,"end":15,"html":"<p>Identity, from <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stat</code>: PID, parent PID, process group, session, the command name (up to 255 characters, though the kernel itself cuts names at 15), the state letter plus its description, and the thread count.</p>"},{"start":16,"end":22,"html":"<p>Memory figures, all in kB as the kernel reports them. <code>unsigned long</code> because sizes are never negative and can be large.</p>"},{"start":23,"end":27,"html":"<p>CPU time in clock ticks: <code>utime</code> (running the program's code) and <code>stime</code> (in the kernel on its behalf). <code>starttime</code> is the start time in ticks after boot, so it needs <code>unsigned long long</code>.</p>"},{"start":28,"end":31,"html":"<p>The derived values in seconds: total CPU time, and how long the process has existed.</p>"},{"start":32,"end":34,"html":"<p>Number of entries in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd/</code>.</p>"},{"start":35,"end":40,"html":"<p><code>cmdline</code> is heap-allocated because arguments can be any length. The two paths are fixed <code>PATH_MAX</code> buffers inside the struct. That makes <code>ProcInfo</code> about 8 KB, which is fine on the stack.</p>"},{"start":41,"end":54,"html":"<p>The API: fill a snapshot, free it, describe a state letter, plus the three builtins.</p>"},{"start":55,"end":55,"html":"<p>End of the include guard.</p>"}]},{"path":"Makefile","title":"How the shell, the tests and the demo programs are built.","group":"Build & tests","lang":"make","lines":["CC ?= gcc","CFLAGS ?= -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -std=c11 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -D_GNU_SOURCE -Iinclude","LDFLAGS ?=","","SRC_DIR = src","INC_DIR = include","BUILD_DIR = build","BIN_DIR = bin","TEST_DIR = tests","EXAMPLE_DIR = examples","","SRCS = $(wildcard $(SRC_DIR)/*.c)","OBJS = $(patsubst $(SRC_DIR)/%.c, $(BUILD_DIR)/%.o, $(SRCS))","DEPS = $(OBJS:.o=.d)","","TARGET = $(BIN_DIR)/forge","TEST_PARSER = $(BIN_DIR)/test_parser","EXAMPLES = $(BIN_DIR)/spin $(BIN_DIR)/leak $(BIN_DIR)/sigtrap","",".PHONY: all debug asan test clean help examples","","all: CFLAGS += -O2","all: $(TARGET) $(TEST_PARSER) $(EXAMPLES)","","debug: CFLAGS += -g3 -O0 -DDEBUG","debug: clean all","","asan: CFLAGS += -g3 -O0 -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -DDEBUG","asan: LDFLAGS += -fsanitize=address,undefined","asan: clean all","","$(TARGET): $(OBJS) | $(BIN_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(OBJS) -o $@ $(LDFLAGS)","","$(TEST_PARSER): $(BUILD_DIR)/test_parser.o $(BUILD_DIR)/parser.o $(BUILD_DIR)/common.o | $(BIN_DIR)","\t$(CC) $^ -o $@ $(LDFLAGS)","","$(BUILD_DIR)/test_parser.o: $(TEST_DIR)/test_parser.c | $(BUILD_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c $< -o $@","","$(BIN_DIR)/spin: $(EXAMPLE_DIR)/spin.c | $(BIN_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $< -o $@ $(LDFLAGS)","","$(BIN_DIR)/leak: $(EXAMPLE_DIR)/leak.c | $(BIN_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $< -o $@ $(LDFLAGS)","","$(BIN_DIR)/sigtrap: $(EXAMPLE_DIR)/sigtrap.c | $(BIN_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $< -o $@ $(LDFLAGS)","","examples: $(EXAMPLES)","","$(BUILD_DIR)/%.o: $(SRC_DIR)/%.c | $(BUILD_DIR)","\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -MMD -MP -c $< -o $@","","$(BUILD_DIR):","\tmkdir -p $(BUILD_DIR)","","$(BIN_DIR):","\tmkdir -p $(BIN_DIR)","","test: all","\t@echo \"Running ForgeShell automated test suite...\"","\t@python3 tests/test_runner.py","","clean:","\trm -rf $(BUILD_DIR) $(BIN_DIR) tests/temp","","help:","\t@echo \"ForgeShell Build Targets:\"","\t@echo \"  make        - Build forge shell, test binaries, and example workloads\"","\t@echo \"  make debug  - Build debug binary with symbols and DEBUG macro\"","\t@echo \"  make asan   - Build with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer\"","\t@echo \"  make test   - Run automated test suite (26 checks)\"","\t@echo \"  make clean  - Remove all build and test artifacts\"","","-include $(DEPS)"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":4,"html":"<p><code>?=</code> sets a value only if it isn't already set, so <code>CC=clang make</code> builds with Clang. The CI runs both compilers this way.</p><p>The flags: <code>-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic</code> turn on most warnings and strict ISO checking. <code>-std=c11</code> is the language version. The two <code>-D</code> feature macros make POSIX and GNU functions visible (see <code>common.h</code>). <code>-Iinclude</code> lets sources write <code>#include \"parser.h\"</code>.</p>"},{"start":5,"end":11,"html":"<p>Folder names in one place: sources, headers, object files (<code>build/</code>), binaries (<code>bin/</code>).</p>"},{"start":12,"end":15,"html":"<p>Every <code>.c</code> in <code>src/</code> is part of the shell automatically, so a new file needs no Makefile change. <code>patsubst</code> maps <code>src/x.c</code> to <code>build/x.o</code>. <code>DEPS</code> lists the matching <code>.d</code> dependency files (see <code>-MMD</code> below).</p>"},{"start":16,"end":19,"html":"<p>Outputs: the shell, the parser unit-test binary, and the three demo programs.</p>"},{"start":20,"end":21,"html":"<p><code>.PHONY</code> marks targets that aren't files. Without it, a file called <code>test</code> in the folder would make <code>make test</code> think there was nothing to do.</p>"},{"start":22,"end":24,"html":"<p><strong>Target-specific variables.</strong> <code>all: CFLAGS += -O2</code> adds optimization only when building the default target. That optimization is exactly what deleted the unused <code>malloc</code> in <code>examples/leak.c</code>. See that file for the story.</p>"},{"start":25,"end":27,"html":"<p><code>make debug</code>: full debug info (<code>-g3</code>), no optimization (<code>-O0</code>, so the debugger matches the source line by line), and <code>DEBUG</code> defined so <code>forge_debug()</code> prints. It runs <code>clean</code> first, so no optimized object files get mixed in.</p>"},{"start":28,"end":31,"html":"<p><code>make asan</code> builds with <strong>AddressSanitizer</strong> (catches out-of-bounds access, use-after-free and leaks at runtime) and <strong>UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer</strong> (catches signed overflow, bad shifts and misaligned access). The sanitizers must be passed to the linker too, hence <code>LDFLAGS</code>. CI runs the whole test suite against this build with both compilers.</p>"},{"start":32,"end":34,"html":"<p>Linking the shell. <code>$@</code> is the target name. Everything after <code>|</code> is an <em>order-only</em> prerequisite: <code>bin/</code> must exist, but its timestamp changing doesn't force a relink.</p>"},{"start":35,"end":37,"html":"<p>The parser test links only what it needs: the test, the parser and the common helpers. No executor, no <code>main.c</code>. That's possible because the parser doesn't depend on the rest of the shell. <code>$^</code> means all the prerequisites.</p>"},{"start":38,"end":40,"html":"<p>Compiles the test file. <code>$&lt;</code> is the first prerequisite.</p>"},{"start":41,"end":49,"html":"<p>The demo programs are single files, compiled and linked in one step.</p>"},{"start":50,"end":51,"html":"<p><code>make examples</code> builds just the demo programs.</p>"},{"start":52,"end":54,"html":"<p>The pattern rule for every source file. <code>-MMD -MP</code> makes the compiler write a <code>.d</code> file listing which headers each <code>.c</code> included. Those are pulled in by <code>-include $(DEPS)</code> at the bottom, so editing <code>jobs.h</code> rebuilds every file that includes it. Without this, Make would only notice changes to <code>.c</code> files.</p>"},{"start":55,"end":60,"html":"<p>Create the output folders on demand.</p>"},{"start":61,"end":64,"html":"<p><code>make test</code> builds everything, then runs the Python integration suite, which in turn runs the C parser tests.</p>"},{"start":65,"end":67,"html":"<p>Removes all build output and the tests' scratch folder.</p>"},{"start":68,"end":75,"html":"<p>A short reference to the targets.</p>"},{"start":76,"end":76,"html":"<p>Pulls in the generated dependency files. The leading <code>-</code> ignores missing files, which is the normal state on a fresh checkout before the first build.</p>"}]},{"path":"tests/test_parser.c","title":"Unit tests for the tokenizer and parser, in plain C with assert().","group":"Build & tests","lang":"c","lines":["#include \"parser.h\"","#include <assert.h>","","static int g_tests_run = 0;","static int g_tests_passed = 0;","","#define TEST(name) \\","    do { \\","        printf(\"  [RUN ] %s\\n\", name); \\","        g_tests_run++; \\","    } while (0)","","#define PASS(name) \\","    do { \\","        printf(\"  [PASS] %s\\n\", name); \\","        g_tests_passed++; \\","    } while (0)","","static void test_simple_command(void) {","    TEST(\"test_simple_command\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"ls\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(!p->is_background);","    assert(p->first_cmd != NULL);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 1);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"ls\") == 0);","    assert(p->first_cmd->next == NULL);","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_simple_command\");","}","","static void test_arguments_and_spaces(void) {","    TEST(\"test_arguments_and_spaces\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"   echo    one   two    three   \");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(!p->is_background);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 4);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"echo\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[1], \"one\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[2], \"two\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[3], \"three\") == 0);","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_arguments_and_spaces\");","}","","static void test_single_quotes(void) {","    TEST(\"test_single_quotes\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"echo 'hello   world   $NOT_EXPANDED'\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"echo\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[1], \"hello   world   $NOT_EXPANDED\") == 0);","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_single_quotes\");","}","","static void test_double_quotes_and_escapes(void) {","    TEST(\"test_double_quotes_and_escapes\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"echo \\\"hello \\\\\\\"quoted\\\\\\\" world\\\"\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"echo\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[1], \"hello \\\"quoted\\\" world\") == 0);","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_double_quotes_and_escapes\");","}","","static void test_redirections(void) {","    TEST(\"test_redirections\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"sort < input.txt > output.txt 2> error.log\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 1);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"sort\") == 0);","    assert(p->first_cmd->input_file != NULL);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->input_file, \"input.txt\") == 0);","    assert(p->first_cmd->output_file != NULL);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->output_file, \"output.txt\") == 0);","    assert(!p->first_cmd->append_output);","    assert(p->first_cmd->error_file != NULL);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->error_file, \"error.log\") == 0);","    free_pipeline(p);","","    /* Test append redirection */","    JobPipeline *p2 = parse_pipeline(\"log_tool >> append.log\");","    assert(p2 != NULL);","    assert(p2->first_cmd->output_file != NULL);","    assert(strcmp(p2->first_cmd->output_file, \"append.log\") == 0);","    assert(p2->first_cmd->append_output);","    free_pipeline(p2);","    PASS(\"test_redirections\");","}","","static void test_pipeline(void) {","    TEST(\"test_pipeline\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"cat file.txt | grep error | wc -l\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(!p->is_background);","","    Command *cmd1 = p->first_cmd;","    assert(cmd1 != NULL);","    assert(cmd1->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(cmd1->argv[0], \"cat\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(cmd1->argv[1], \"file.txt\") == 0);","","    Command *cmd2 = cmd1->next;","    assert(cmd2 != NULL);","    assert(cmd2->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(cmd2->argv[0], \"grep\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(cmd2->argv[1], \"error\") == 0);","","    Command *cmd3 = cmd2->next;","    assert(cmd3 != NULL);","    assert(cmd3->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(cmd3->argv[0], \"wc\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(cmd3->argv[1], \"-l\") == 0);","    assert(cmd3->next == NULL);","","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_pipeline\");","}","","static void test_background_job(void) {","    TEST(\"test_background_job\");","    JobPipeline *p = parse_pipeline(\"sleep 100 &\");","    assert(p != NULL);","    assert(p->is_background);","    assert(p->first_cmd->argc == 2);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[0], \"sleep\") == 0);","    assert(strcmp(p->first_cmd->argv[1], \"100\") == 0);","    free_pipeline(p);","    PASS(\"test_background_job\");","}","","static void test_syntax_errors(void) {","    TEST(\"test_syntax_errors\");","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"   \\t  \\n\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"| echo hello\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"echo hello |\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"cat <\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"cat >\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"cat 2>\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"echo 'unclosed quote\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"echo \\\"unclosed quote\") == NULL);","    assert(parse_pipeline(\"ls | > out.txt\") == NULL);","    PASS(\"test_syntax_errors\");","}","","int main(void) {","    printf(\"\\n=== ForgeShell Parser Unit Tests ===\\n\");","    test_simple_command();","    test_arguments_and_spaces();","    test_single_quotes();","    test_double_quotes_and_escapes();","    test_redirections();","    test_pipeline();","    test_background_job();","    test_syntax_errors();","","    printf(\"\\nSummary: %d / %d tests passed successfully.\\n\\n\", g_tests_passed, g_tests_run);","    return (g_tests_passed == g_tests_run) ? 0 : 1;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p>No test framework: <code>assert()</code> aborts the program with the failing expression and its line number, which is enough here. Two counters track how many tests ran and passed.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":18,"html":"<p>Small macros that print <code>[RUN ]</code> and <code>[PASS]</code> lines. The <code>do { ... } while (0)</code> wrapper is the standard trick that makes a multi-statement macro behave like one statement, so it's safe after an <code>if</code> without braces.</p>"},{"start":19,"end":31,"html":"<p>The simplest case: one word gives one command, <code>argc == 1</code>, not background, and no next command. Every test frees its pipeline, so running the tests under AddressSanitizer also checks the parser for leaks.</p>"},{"start":32,"end":45,"html":"<p>Extra spaces at the start, middle and end vanish: four words, four arguments.</p>"},{"start":46,"end":56,"html":"<p>Inside single quotes, spaces and <code>$</code> are kept exactly: <code>'hello   world   $NOT_EXPANDED'</code> becomes one argument.</p>"},{"start":57,"end":67,"html":"<p>Inside double quotes, <code>\\\"</code> produces a literal quote. The C string is hard to read because it's escaped twice, once for C and once for the shell. The shell sees <code>echo \"hello \\\"quoted\\\" world\"</code>.</p>"},{"start":68,"end":92,"html":"<p>All three redirects on one command, and the append flag for <code>&gt;&gt;</code>. Note that <code>sort</code> is still <code>argc == 1</code>: redirect targets aren't arguments.</p>"},{"start":93,"end":121,"html":"<p>A three-stage pipeline becomes a linked list of three commands, each with its own arguments, and the last <code>next</code> is <code>NULL</code>.</p>"},{"start":122,"end":133,"html":"<p>A trailing <code>&amp;</code> sets <code>is_background</code> and isn't itself an argument.</p>"},{"start":134,"end":148,"html":"<p>Each invalid input must return <code>NULL</code>: empty or blank lines, a pipe at the start or end, redirects with no file name, unclosed quotes, and (added with today's fix) a pipeline stage with no command, <code>ls | &gt; out.txt</code>. These cases also print error messages, which is expected output.</p>"},{"start":149,"end":162,"html":"<p>Runs every test. Because a failing <code>assert</code> aborts the program, reaching the summary means all of them passed. The exit status (0 or 1) is what <code>tests/test_runner.py</code> checks.</p>"}]},{"path":"tests/test_runner.py","title":"The integration test suite: runs the real bin/forge binary with scripted input and checks what comes out.","group":"Build & tests","lang":"python","lines":["#!/usr/bin/env python3","import subprocess","import os","import sys","import time","import shutil","import tempfile","","REPO_ROOT = os.path.abspath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), \"..\"))","BIN_FORGE = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"bin\", \"forge\")","BIN_TEST_PARSER = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"bin\", \"test_parser\")","BIN_SPIN = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"bin\", \"spin\")","BIN_LEAK = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"bin\", \"leak\")","BIN_SIGTRAP = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"bin\", \"sigtrap\")","","TEMP_DIR = os.path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"tests\", \"temp\")","","GREEN = \"\\033[32m\"","RED = \"\\033[31m\"","BOLD = \"\\033[1m\"","RESET = \"\\033[0m\"","","passed = 0","failed = 0","","def run_test(name, fn):","    global passed, failed","    sys.stdout.write(f\"  [TEST] {name} ... \")","    sys.stdout.flush()","    try:","        fn()","        print(f\"{GREEN}PASSED{RESET}\")","        passed += 1","    except AssertionError as e:","        print(f\"{RED}FAILED{RESET} (AssertionError: {e})\")","        failed += 1","    except Exception as e:","        print(f\"{RED}ERROR{RESET} ({e})\")","        failed += 1","","def run_shell(input_text):","    proc = subprocess.Popen(","        [BIN_FORGE],","        stdin=subprocess.PIPE,","        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,","        stderr=subprocess.PIPE,","        text=True,","        cwd=TEMP_DIR","    )","    stdout, stderr = proc.communicate(input=input_text)","    return proc.returncode, stdout, stderr","","# ==========================================","# 1. Process Execution Tests","# ==========================================","def test_normal_exit():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"echo hello world\\nexit 0\\n\")","    assert code == 0, f\"Expected 0, got {code}\"","    assert \"hello world\" in out, f\"Output missing 'hello world': {out}\"","","def test_nonzero_exit():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"exit 42\\n\")","    assert code == 42, f\"Expected 42, got {code}\"","","def test_command_not_found():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"this_command_does_not_exist_xyz123\\n\")","    assert \"command not found\" in err or \"command not found\" in out","    assert code == 127, f\"Expected 127 for not found, got {code}\"","","def test_child_crash_signal():","    # Trigger SIGSEGV in child via perl or bash","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"/bin/sh -c 'kill -SEGV $$'\\n\")","    assert code == 128 + 11, f\"Expected 139 (128+SIGSEGV), got {code}\"","    assert \"Segmentation fault\" in err or \"Segmentation fault\" in out","","def test_zombie_prevention():","    script = \"\"\"","for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do","  /bin/sh -c 'exit 0' &","done","sleep 0.3","jobs","exit 0","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert code == 0","","# ==========================================","# 2. Parser & Quoting Tests","# ==========================================","def test_c_unit_parser():","    res = subprocess.run([BIN_TEST_PARSER], capture_output=True, text=True)","    assert res.returncode == 0, f\"C parser tests failed:\\n{res.stdout}\\n{res.stderr}\"","","def test_shell_quoting():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"echo 'single   spaces   preserved'\\necho \\\"double   spaces\\\"\\n\")","    assert \"single   spaces   preserved\" in out","    assert \"double   spaces\" in out","","# ==========================================","# 3. Redirection Tests","# ==========================================","def test_stdout_redirection():","    out_file = os.path.join(TEMP_DIR, \"out.txt\")","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"echo 'forge redirection test' > out.txt\\n\")","    assert os.path.exists(out_file)","    with open(out_file, \"r\") as f:","        content = f.read().strip()","    assert content == \"forge redirection test\", f\"Got: {content}\"","","def test_stdin_redirection():","    in_file = os.path.join(TEMP_DIR, \"in.txt\")","    with open(in_file, \"w\") as f:","        f.write(\"input file line content\\n\")","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"cat < in.txt\\n\")","    assert \"input file line content\" in out","","def test_append_redirection():","    app_file = os.path.join(TEMP_DIR, \"app.txt\")","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"echo line1 > app.txt\\necho line2 >> app.txt\\n\")","    assert os.path.exists(app_file)","    with open(app_file, \"r\") as f:","        lines = f.read().splitlines()","    assert lines == [\"line1\", \"line2\"], f\"Got: {lines}\"","","def test_stderr_redirection():","    err_file = os.path.join(TEMP_DIR, \"err.txt\")","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"ls non_existent_file_xyz_999 2> err.txt\\n\")","    assert os.path.exists(err_file)","    with open(err_file, \"r\") as f:","        content = f.read()","    assert \"No such file or directory\" in content or \"cannot access\" in content","","# ==========================================","# 4. Pipeline Tests","# ==========================================","def test_single_pipe():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"printf 'banana\\\\napple\\\\ncherry\\\\n' | grep apple\\n\")","    assert out.strip() == \"apple\", f\"Got: {out}\"","","def test_multiple_pipes():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"printf 'cat\\\\ndog\\\\nbird\\\\nfish\\\\n' | grep -v dog | sort\\n\")","    lines = [l.strip() for l in out.strip().splitlines() if l.strip()]","    assert lines == [\"bird\", \"cat\", \"fish\"], f\"Got: {lines}\"","","def test_large_pipeline_no_deadlock():","    # Stream 30,000 lines through pipe chain","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"yes 'systems programming' | head -n 30000 | wc -l\\n\")","    assert \"30000\" in out, f\"Expected 30000 in output, got: {out}\"","","def test_pipeline_exit_status():","    # A pipeline's status is its last command's, whichever process exits last.","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"sleep 0.3 | false\\n\")","    assert code == 1, f\"Expected 1 from 'sleep | false', got {code}\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"false | sleep 0.3\\n\")","    assert code == 0, f\"Expected 0 from 'false | sleep', got {code}\"","","def test_descriptor_closure():","    # Verify file descriptors are not leaked after multiple commands","    script = \"\"\"","ls > /dev/null","ls | cat > /dev/null","ls | cat | grep x > /dev/null","ls -1 /proc/self/fd | wc -l","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert code == 0","    # Process should have at most standard 0, 1, 2 and proc dir fd","    num_fds = int(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])","    assert num_fds <= 5, f\"Unexpected high number of file descriptors: {num_fds}\"","","# ==========================================","# 5. Builtins Tests","# ==========================================","def test_builtin_pwd_and_cd():","    script = \"\"\"","pwd","cd /tmp","pwd","cd -","pwd","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert code == 0","    lines = [l.strip() for l in out.strip().splitlines() if l.strip()]","    assert \"/tmp\" in lines","","def test_builtin_export_and_unset():","    script = \"\"\"","export TEST_VAR=ForgeShellSpecial","/bin/sh -c 'echo val=$TEST_VAR'","unset TEST_VAR","/bin/sh -c 'echo val=$TEST_VAR'","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert \"val=ForgeShellSpecial\" in out","    assert \"val=\" in out.split(\"val=ForgeShellSpecial\")[1]","","def test_builtin_help():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"help\\n\")","    assert \"ForgeShell\" in out","    assert \"Builtin Commands:\" in out","","# ==========================================","# 6. Job Control Tests","# ==========================================","def test_background_job_and_reaping():","    script = \"\"\"","sleep 0.2 &","jobs","sleep 0.4","jobs","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert \"Running\" in out","    assert \"Done\" in out","","def test_job_numbering():","    # Foreground commands must not consume job numbers.","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"/bin/true\\n/bin/true\\nsleep 0.3 &\\nsleep 0.5\\n\")","    assert \"[1] \" in out, f\"Expected first background job to be [1], got: {out}\"","","def test_fg_builtin():","    script = \"\"\"","sleep 0.2 &","fg 1","\"\"\"","    code, out, err = run_shell(script)","    assert code == 0","","# ==========================================","# 7. Linux /proc Observability Tests","# ==========================================","def test_proc_inspect():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"inspect 1\\n\")","    assert \"Process Inspection Report for PID 1\" in out","    assert \"Virtual Size (VmSize):\" in out","    assert \"Resident Set (VmRSS):\" in out","    assert \"Source: /proc/1/stat\" in out","    assert \"Source: /proc/1/status\" in out","","def test_proc_inspect_missing():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"inspect 9999999\\n\")","    assert \"not found or terminated\" in err or \"not found or terminated\" in out","","def test_proc_ps():","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"ps | head -n 5\\n\")","    assert \"PID\" in out","    assert \"STAT\" in out","    assert \"COMMAND\" in out","","def test_proc_monitor_termination():","    script = \"\"\"","sleep 0.5 &","monitor 1 1","\"\"\"","    # monitor PID 1 for 1 loop then exit or monitor self","    code, out, err = run_shell(\"sleep 0.2 &\\n\")","    assert code == 0","","def main():","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}=== ForgeShell Automated Systems Test Suite ==={RESET}\\n\")","","    os.makedirs(TEMP_DIR, exist_ok=True)","","    print(f\"{BOLD}[Category 1: Process Execution]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Normal exit (0)\", test_normal_exit)","    run_test(\"Non-zero exit\", test_nonzero_exit)","    run_test(\"Command not found (127)\", test_command_not_found)","    run_test(\"Child crash signal (SIGSEGV)\", test_child_crash_signal)","    run_test(\"Zombie child prevention\", test_zombie_prevention)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 2: Parser & Quoting]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"C Unit Tests (test_parser)\", test_c_unit_parser)","    run_test(\"Shell quoting and escapes\", test_shell_quoting)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 3: Redirections]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Stdout redirection (>)\", test_stdout_redirection)","    run_test(\"Stdin redirection (<)\", test_stdin_redirection)","    run_test(\"Append redirection (>>)\", test_append_redirection)","    run_test(\"Stderr redirection (2>)\", test_stderr_redirection)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 4: Pipelines]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Single pipe (cmd1 | cmd2)\", test_single_pipe)","    run_test(\"Multi-pipe chain (3 stages)\", test_multiple_pipes)","    run_test(\"Large stream buffer (30k lines)\", test_large_pipeline_no_deadlock)","    run_test(\"Pipeline exit status (last command)\", test_pipeline_exit_status)","    run_test(\"File descriptor leak verification\", test_descriptor_closure)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 5: Builtins]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Builtin pwd & cd (with -)\", test_builtin_pwd_and_cd)","    run_test(\"Builtin export & unset\", test_builtin_export_and_unset)","    run_test(\"Builtin help\", test_builtin_help)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 6: Job Control]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Background jobs (&, jobs, reap)\", test_background_job_and_reaping)","    run_test(\"Job numbering starts at [1]\", test_job_numbering)","    run_test(\"Builtin fg foreground transfer\", test_fg_builtin)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}[Category 7: Linux /proc Observability]{RESET}\")","    run_test(\"Builtin inspect <pid>\", test_proc_inspect)","    run_test(\"Builtin inspect invalid PID\", test_proc_inspect_missing)","    run_test(\"Builtin ps process overview\", test_proc_ps)","    run_test(\"Monitor termination handling\", test_proc_monitor_termination)","","    # Clean up temp","    shutil.rmtree(TEMP_DIR, ignore_errors=True)","","    print(f\"\\n{BOLD}Test Summary: {passed} passed, {failed} failed.{RESET}\\n\")","    sys.exit(0 if failed == 0 else 1)","","if __name__ == \"__main__\":","    main()"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":8,"html":"<p>Integration tests treat the shell as a black box: start <code>bin/forge</code>, feed it commands on stdin, and check stdout, stderr, the exit code and any files it created. They test what a user would see, so the same suite works no matter how the C code is organised inside.</p>"},{"start":9,"end":15,"html":"<p>Paths to the binaries under test, worked out from this file's location, so the suite runs from any directory.</p>"},{"start":16,"end":17,"html":"<p>A scratch folder, created fresh and deleted at the end. The shell runs with it as its working directory, so redirect tests write their files there and not into the repository.</p>"},{"start":18,"end":22,"html":"<p>Colors for PASSED and FAILED, the same ANSI codes the shell uses.</p>"},{"start":23,"end":40,"html":"<p>Global counters, plus <code>run_test</code>, which runs one test function and counts it. A failed <code>assert</code> is a test failure. Any other exception, a crash in the test itself, is reported as ERROR so the two are easy to tell apart.</p>"},{"start":41,"end":55,"html":"<p>The key helper. It starts ForgeShell with pipes for stdin, stdout and stderr and sends the whole script at once. <code>communicate()</code> writes the input, closes stdin (which the shell sees as EOF, so it exits) and collects everything printed.</p><p>Because stdin is a pipe, not a terminal, the shell runs <strong>non-interactively</strong>: no prompt, no terminal control, no Ctrl+Z. That makes the tests deterministic, but it also means the interactive parts of job control can't be tested this way. Testing those would need a pseudo-terminal, which is how <code>tools/record_demo.py</code> drives the shell.</p>"},{"start":56,"end":60,"html":"<p><code>exit 0</code> after a command must give exit code 0, and the output must arrive.</p>"},{"start":61,"end":64,"html":"<p><code>exit 42</code> must make the shell's exit code 42, so a shell running a script reports failure correctly.</p>"},{"start":65,"end":69,"html":"<p>An unknown command prints \"command not found\" and gives <strong>127</strong>, the code set in the child after <code>execvp</code> fails with <code>ENOENT</code>.</p>"},{"start":70,"end":75,"html":"<p><code>kill -SEGV $$</code> makes a child shell send itself a segmentation fault. ForgeShell must report it the way every shell does: exit status <strong>139</strong> (128 + 11) and a \"Segmentation fault\" message. This checks the <code>WIFSIGNALED</code> branch.</p>"},{"start":76,"end":90,"html":"<p>Starts five background jobs that exit at once, waits, then runs <code>jobs</code>. The shell must reap them all without crashing or hanging.</p><p>A stronger version would check <code>/proc</code> for zombie (<code>Z</code>) children of the shell. This test mainly checks that reaping doesn't break anything.</p>"},{"start":91,"end":94,"html":"<p>Runs the C parser tests as part of this suite, so <code>make test</code> covers both levels.</p>"},{"start":95,"end":102,"html":"<p>Quoting end to end: spaces inside both kinds of quotes survive all the way to <code>echo</code>'s output.</p>"},{"start":103,"end":117,"html":"<p>Each redirect test runs a command, then opens the resulting file from Python to check its contents. This covers <code>&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;</code>.</p>"},{"start":118,"end":125,"html":"<p><code>&gt;</code> then <code>&gt;&gt;</code> must leave exactly two lines. A mistake like using <code>O_TRUNC</code> for <code>&gt;&gt;</code> would leave one.</p>"},{"start":126,"end":136,"html":"<p><code>ls</code> on a missing file writes its complaint to stderr, and <code>2&gt;</code> must capture it in the file. The check accepts both GNU and BSD wording.</p>"},{"start":137,"end":140,"html":"<p>A pipe in the middle of a command: <code>printf | grep</code>.</p>"},{"start":141,"end":145,"html":"<p>A three-stage pipeline, which exercises the <code>prev_pipe_read</code> hand-off between loop iterations.</p>"},{"start":146,"end":150,"html":"<p>30,000 lines through <code>yes | head | wc</code>. A pipe holds only 64 KB by default, so this only completes if the stages really run at the same time, and if <code>yes</code> gets <code>SIGPIPE</code> when <code>head</code> exits. If a pipe end were left open somewhere, <code>wc</code> would never see EOF and the test would hang.</p>"},{"start":151,"end":157,"html":"<p>Added with the exit-status fix. <code>sleep 0.3 | false</code> must exit 1 even though <code>sleep</code> finishes <em>after</em> <code>false</code>, and the reverse order must exit 0. Testing both orders proves the status comes from the last <em>command</em>, not from whichever process finished last.</p>"},{"start":158,"end":174,"html":"<p>After several pipelines, <code>ls -1 /proc/self/fd | wc -l</code> counts the descriptors a <em>child</em> inherited from the shell. If the shell were leaking pipe ends, this number would grow. The limit of 5 allows for 0, 1, 2, the directory <code>ls</code> is reading, and a little slack.</p>"},{"start":175,"end":187,"html":"<p><code>cd /tmp</code>, <code>pwd</code>, then <code>cd -</code> back. This checks that <code>cd</code> ran <em>inside</em> the shell process: if it had run in a child, the next <code>pwd</code> wouldn't show <code>/tmp</code>.</p>"},{"start":188,"end":198,"html":"<p><code>export</code> must reach a <em>child</em> process, checked with <code>/bin/sh -c 'echo $TEST_VAR'</code>, and <code>unset</code> must remove it again.</p>"},{"start":199,"end":206,"html":"<p><code>help</code> prints its reference.</p>"},{"start":207,"end":217,"html":"<p>A short background job shows as Running, then as Done after it finishes. That's the whole reap-and-report path in <code>check_and_notify_jobs</code>.</p>"},{"start":218,"end":222,"html":"<p>Added with the job-ID fix: two foreground commands first, then a background job that must be <code>[1]</code>. Before the fix it was <code>[3]</code>.</p>"},{"start":223,"end":233,"html":"<p><code>fg 1</code> on a background job must wait for it and return 0. Without a terminal this tests the waiting, not the terminal hand-off.</p>"},{"start":234,"end":241,"html":"<p><code>inspect 1</code> reads PID 1 (init or systemd), which always exists. The test checks that the report cites its <code>/proc</code> sources.</p>"},{"start":242,"end":245,"html":"<p>A PID that can't exist must give a clean error, not a crash.</p>"},{"start":246,"end":251,"html":"<p><code>ps | head -n 5</code> checks the header and also exercises a builtin inside a pipeline (it runs in a forked child).</p>"},{"start":252,"end":260,"html":"<p>Honest note: despite its name, this test only checks that the shell starts a background job and exits cleanly. It never runs <code>monitor</code>, whose loop needs Ctrl+C or the watched process to exit. A real test could start <code>sleep 1 &amp;</code>, run <code>monitor &lt;pid&gt;</code> and check for \"has terminated\".</p>"},{"start":261,"end":313,"html":"<p>Creates the scratch folder, runs every test by category, deletes the folder, and exits with 0 only if nothing failed. That exit code is what makes CI go red or green.</p>"}]},{"path":"examples/spin.c","title":"Demo program: sleeps or burns CPU for a while, so there's something to watch with jobs, monitor and Ctrl+Z.","group":"Demo programs","lang":"c","lines":["#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L","#include <stdio.h>","#include <stdlib.h>","#include <unistd.h>","#include <signal.h>","#include <stdbool.h>","","static volatile sig_atomic_t g_running = 1;","","static void sig_handler(int sig) {","    if (sig == SIGINT) {","        printf(\"\\n[spin] Received SIGINT (PID %d), exiting gracefully.\\n\", (int)getpid());","        g_running = 0;","    } else if (sig == SIGTERM) {","        printf(\"\\n[spin] Received SIGTERM (PID %d), exiting.\\n\", (int)getpid());","        g_running = 0;","    }","}","","int main(int argc, char **argv) {","    signal(SIGINT, sig_handler);","    signal(SIGTERM, sig_handler);","","    int seconds = (argc > 1) ? atoi(argv[1]) : 10;","    bool burn_cpu = (argc > 2 && atoi(argv[2]) == 1);","","    printf(\"[spin] Started PID %d (duration: %ds, mode: %s)\\n\",","           (int)getpid(), seconds, burn_cpu ? \"burn CPU\" : \"sleep\");","    fflush(stdout);","","    for (int i = 0; i < seconds && g_running; i++) {","        if (burn_cpu) {","            /* Burn CPU for ~1 second */","            for (volatile unsigned long j = 0; j < 250000000UL && g_running; j++) {","                /* busy loop */","            }","        } else {","            sleep(1);","        }","    }","","    printf(\"[spin] PID %d finished.\\n\", (int)getpid());","    return 0;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":9,"html":"<p><code>_POSIX_C_SOURCE</code> makes <code>sleep</code>, <code>getpid</code> and <code>sig_atomic_t</code> visible under <code>-std=c11</code>. <code>g_running</code> is the usual handler-to-main-loop flag.</p>"},{"start":10,"end":19,"html":"<p>Handles Ctrl+C and <code>kill</code> by printing a message and letting the loop end, which shows that a program can <em>catch</em> <code>SIGINT</code> and exit politely instead of dying.</p><p>Calling <code>printf</code> in a handler isn't async-signal-safe (see <code>src/signals.c</code>). For a demo program that's only interrupted while sleeping or spinning it works in practice, but production code would use <code>write()</code> or just set the flag.</p>"},{"start":20,"end":30,"html":"<p><code>spin [count] [mode]</code>: <code>count</code> rounds (default 10). Mode <code>1</code> means burn CPU, otherwise sleep a second per round.</p><p><code>signal()</code> here, rather than <code>sigaction</code>, is fine for a demo. Note what's <em>missing</em>: there's no <code>SIGTSTP</code> handler, so Ctrl+Z uses the default action and stops the process, which is exactly what the job-control demo needs.</p>"},{"start":31,"end":41,"html":"<p>In CPU mode, each round is 250 million iterations of an empty loop on a <code>volatile</code> counter. <code>volatile</code> forces every increment to really happen, otherwise <code>-O2</code> would delete the loop as useless. A round is <em>not</em> a second: on a modern CPU it takes about 0.1 s, which is why the demo uses <code>spin 600 1</code>. <code>monitor</code> shows this mode at about 100% CPU, one full core.</p>"},{"start":42,"end":44,"html":"<p>Prints a final line so you can see in the terminal when a background <code>spin</code> finishes.</p>"}]},{"path":"examples/leak.c","title":"Demo program: grabs N megabytes, touches every page, and holds them, so inspect and monitor have memory to show.","group":"Demo programs","lang":"c","lines":["#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L","#include <stdio.h>","#include <stdlib.h>","#include <string.h>","#include <unistd.h>","","int main(int argc, char **argv) {","    int mb = (argc > 1) ? atoi(argv[1]) : 20;","    int hold_sec = (argc > 2) ? atoi(argv[2]) : 5;","","    printf(\"[leak] Started PID %d: allocating %d MB and holding for %ds...\\n\",","           (int)getpid(), mb, hold_sec);","    fflush(stdout);","","    size_t bytes = (size_t)mb * 1024 * 1024;","    /* volatile: the writes below are never read back, so without it an","     * optimizing compiler (-O2) is allowed to delete the malloc, the writes","     * and the free entirely, and the process would never grow. */","    volatile char *buf = malloc(bytes);","    if (!buf) {","        perror(\"malloc\");","        return 1;","    }","","    /* Touch every page so the kernel actually backs it with RAM (VmRSS) */","    for (size_t i = 0; i < bytes; i += 4096) {","        buf[i] = 0x42;","    }","","    printf(\"[leak] Memory touched. Check VmRSS in /proc/%d/status now!\\n\", (int)getpid());","    fflush(stdout);","","    sleep(hold_sec);","","    free((void *)buf);","    printf(\"[leak] PID %d done.\\n\", (int)getpid());","    return 0;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":6,"html":"<p>Standard headers. No signal handling: <code>leak</code> just sleeps and exits.</p>"},{"start":7,"end":14,"html":"<p><code>leak [MB] [seconds]</code> defaults to 20 MB held for 5 seconds. It prints its PID straight away so you can run <code>inspect &lt;PID&gt;</code> from another terminal.</p>"},{"start":15,"end":24,"html":"<p><code>(size_t)</code> <em>before</em> multiplying matters: <code>mb * 1024 * 1024</code> in <code>int</code> overflows above 2047 MB.</p><p><strong>A bug fixed today.</strong> This used to be <code>char *buf = malloc(bytes); memset(buf, 0x42, bytes); ... free(buf);</code>. The program never <em>reads</em> the memory, so under <code>-O2</code> GCC was allowed to delete the <code>malloc</code>, the <code>memset</code> and the <code>free</code> entirely, since they have no visible effect. The shell's <code>inspect</code> showed 2 MB instead of 300 MB, and that's how the bug was found. Declaring the pointer <code>volatile</code> means every write counts as an observable effect, so the compiler must keep them.</p>"},{"start":25,"end":29,"html":"<p><code>malloc</code> of a large block only reserves address space: VmSize grows, but no RAM is used yet. The kernel gives out physical pages lazily, on the first write to each 4 KB page (a <em>page fault</em>). Writing one byte per page is enough to make all of it resident, so VmRSS climbs to about 300 MB. That's the difference between VmSize and VmRSS, shown live.</p>"},{"start":30,"end":34,"html":"<p>Then hold the memory for the requested time. <code>sleep</code> puts the process in state <code>S</code>, so it uses no CPU while you inspect it.</p>"},{"start":35,"end":38,"html":"<p><code>free</code> takes a plain <code>void *</code>, so the <code>volatile</code> qualifier is cast away here. Freeing just before exit doesn't change much, since the kernel takes back all of a process's memory when it ends, but it keeps leak checkers like AddressSanitizer quiet.</p>"}]},{"path":"examples/sigtrap.c","title":"Demo program: catches Ctrl+C and Ctrl+Z instead of dying, to show signal delivery from the shell.","group":"Demo programs","lang":"c","lines":["#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L","#include <stdio.h>","#include <stdlib.h>","#include <unistd.h>","#include <signal.h>","","static volatile sig_atomic_t g_count = 0;","","static void sig_handler(int sig) {","    if (sig == SIGINT) {","        printf(\"[sigtrap] Interrupted with SIGINT! Count: %d\\n\", ++g_count);","        fflush(stdout);","        if (g_count >= 2) {","            printf(\"[sigtrap] Exiting after 2 SIGINTs.\\n\");","            exit(0);","        }","    } else if (sig == SIGTSTP) {","        printf(\"[sigtrap] Caught SIGTSTP! (Terminal suspend signal)\\n\");","        fflush(stdout);","    }","}","","int main(void) {","    signal(SIGINT, sig_handler);","    signal(SIGTSTP, sig_handler);","","    printf(\"[sigtrap] PID %d listening for SIGINT and SIGTSTP. Send signals or Ctrl+C / Ctrl+Z.\\n\", (int)getpid());","    fflush(stdout);","","    while (1) {","        pause();","    }","    return 0;","}"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":8,"html":"<p>Counts how many Ctrl+Cs have arrived, using the signal-safe <code>sig_atomic_t</code> type.</p>"},{"start":9,"end":22,"html":"<p>The first Ctrl+C is caught and counted, the second ends the program. Ctrl+Z is caught too, so the program <em>doesn't</em> stop. Run it in ForgeShell and Ctrl+Z appears to do nothing, which is a good way to show that <code>SIGTSTP</code> is a request a program can refuse. (<code>SIGSTOP</code> can't be caught or ignored.)</p><p>Like <code>spin</code>, it calls <code>printf</code> and <code>exit</code> in a handler, which is fine for a demo but not async-signal-safe.</p>"},{"start":23,"end":34,"html":"<p><code>pause()</code> sleeps until any signal is handled, then returns, and the loop goes back to waiting. The process uses no CPU while waiting.</p><p>To stop it from inside ForgeShell, press Ctrl+C twice, or run <code>kill &lt;pid&gt;</code> from another terminal.</p>"}]},{"path":".github/workflows/ci.yml","title":"Continuous integration: every push is built and tested with two compilers, plain and sanitized.","group":"Build & tests","lang":"yaml","lines":["name: CI","","on:","  push:","    branches: [main, \"feat/**\"]","  pull_request:","  workflow_dispatch:","","jobs:","  build-and-test:","    name: ${{ matrix.compiler }} / ${{ matrix.mode }}","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    strategy:","      fail-fast: false","      matrix:","        compiler: [gcc, clang]","        mode: [release, asan]","    env:","      CC: ${{ matrix.compiler }}","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","","      - name: Build (release)","        if: matrix.mode == 'release'","        run: make","","      - name: Build (AddressSanitizer + UBSan)","        if: matrix.mode == 'asan'","        run: make asan","","      - name: Run test suite","        run: python3 tests/test_runner.py","","  guide:","    name: guide is in sync with the source","    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@v4","","      - name: Check docs/assets/source-data.js","        run: python3 tools/guide/build.py --check"],"blocks":[{"start":1,"end":8,"html":"<p>Runs on pushes to <code>main</code> and feature branches, on pull requests, and on demand (the \"Run workflow\" button in the Actions tab).</p>"},{"start":9,"end":19,"html":"<p>A <strong>matrix</strong> runs four copies of the job: gcc and clang, each in a release build and in an AddressSanitizer + UBSan build. <code>fail-fast: false</code> lets all four finish even if one fails, so you see every combination's result. <code>CC</code> is passed to <code>make</code> through the environment, which works because the Makefile uses <code>CC ?= gcc</code>.</p>"},{"start":20,"end":33,"html":"<p>Check out the code, build the right variant, and run the full Python test suite against it. Under the sanitizers, any memory error or undefined behaviour during the tests aborts the shell, and the test that triggered it fails.</p>"},{"start":34,"end":41,"html":"<p>A second job keeps this guide honest. It rebuilds the annotated source data and fails if the result differs from <code>docs/assets/source-data.js</code>. If code changes and a note's anchor no longer matches, or the data wasn't regenerated, CI goes red.</p>"}]}]};
